WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.992 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.992 --> 00:00:03.486 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.486 --> 00:00:05.478 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.478 --> 00:00:17.730 align:middle line:90%


00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.390 align:middle line:90%
TOBIAS SALZ: OK.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.990 align:middle line:90%
Thanks so much for being here.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.190 align:middle line:84%
I know it's a very busy
time of the semester.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to talk a little
bit more about search models.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.910 align:middle line:84%
And I know that
Glenn has already

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.850 align:middle line:84%
introduced you both to the
theory side and some empirics.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:40.420 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to be brief
and motivating the topic.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.690 align:middle line:84%
So there was a famous
article published

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:48.400 align:middle line:84%
by George Stigler in 1961
where he made the observation

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.060 align:middle line:84%
that in many markets, it's
not the case that there's

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:53.830 align:middle line:84%
a central marketplace
where everybody

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.250 align:middle line:90%
observes the relevant prices.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:59.800 align:middle line:84%
But oftentimes you actually
have to move around,

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.100 align:middle line:84%
go to different places,
incur physical search costs

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.290 align:middle line:90%
to figure out what prices are.

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:09.100 align:middle line:84%
Or maybe it's mentally
costly to figure out

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.550 align:middle line:90%
what the right price is.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.900 align:middle line:84%
And so that
introduces frictions,

00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:16.200 align:middle line:90%
which he termed search.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.400 align:middle line:84%
And so in this
quote here, he says

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.990 align:middle line:84%
that we can look at the
dispersion in prices.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.720 align:middle line:84%
And in some sense,
they're a measure

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.365 align:middle line:84%
of the amount of
ignorance in the market.

00:01:26.365 --> 00:01:29.230 align:middle line:90%


00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.530 align:middle line:84%
So why do we want to
look at economic settings

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.280 align:middle line:84%
through the lens
of a search model?

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, so first of all, there's
several positive arguments

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.140 align:middle line:90%
why we may want to do that.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:42.810 align:middle line:84%
So first of all,
in many settings,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:47.190 align:middle line:84%
we do see that products that
are very similar or essentially

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.860 align:middle line:84%
identical are sold for
very different prices.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.040 align:middle line:84%
And so as researchers,
we would like

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.865 align:middle line:84%
to make sense of that
observation how can this be.

00:01:56.865 --> 00:01:59.796 align:middle line:90%


00:01:59.796 --> 00:02:04.500 align:middle line:84%
And again, so one
potential explanation

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.330 align:middle line:84%
which we will explore
today empirically

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.699 align:middle line:84%
is that with search
frictions, it

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.860 align:middle line:84%
could be that you get a
mixed strategy pricing

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:14.700 align:middle line:84%
equilibrium, which leads to
equilibrium price dispersion.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:19.050 align:middle line:84%
But then within the
theme of empirical IO,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.000 align:middle line:90%
it's also normatively important.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:25.770 align:middle line:84%
So when we quantify, let's
say, welfare in markets,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.610 align:middle line:84%
we might want to take search
frictions into account

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:30.990 align:middle line:84%
foremost because
they're actually

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.850 align:middle line:90%
a source of market power.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:37.050 align:middle line:84%
So in IO, we are predominantly
concerned with various ways

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:38.920 align:middle line:90%
that firms gain market power.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.890 align:middle line:84%
Such frictions are one potential
source of market power.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.290 align:middle line:84%
There are also, depending
on how you think

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:48.010 align:middle line:90%
about them, a social cost.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:52.170 align:middle line:84%
So if people have to
engage in wasteful effort

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.900 align:middle line:84%
to drive around to
various car dealerships

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.190 align:middle line:84%
to figure out what the
right car prices are,

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.260 align:middle line:90%
that's socially costly.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.860 align:middle line:84%
And if we do welfare
calculations,

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.520 align:middle line:84%
we may perhaps want to
account for those costs.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.370 align:middle line:84%
Such costs can also
lead to misallocation.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.410 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
you're looking for,

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.870 align:middle line:84%
let's say, a hotel
for your vacation

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:15.360 align:middle line:84%
or, let's say, a car
or something else,

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.460 align:middle line:84%
you might not, in
fact, find the product

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.270 align:middle line:84%
that you would like to purchase
the most at a given price point.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.150 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, if you think
about a firm, let's say,

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.070 align:middle line:84%
purchasing inputs
from a supplier,

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:27.900 align:middle line:84%
the firm might not
find a supplier

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.410 align:middle line:84%
that can offer the specific
product at the lowest cost.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.770 align:middle line:84%
And so that leads
to welfare losses

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:37.350 align:middle line:84%
that, again, we might
want to quantify.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.782 align:middle line:90%


00:03:40.782 --> 00:03:42.770 align:middle line:90%
So this is general.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:47.090 align:middle line:84%
For the second paper that
I'm going to discuss today,

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:49.940 align:middle line:84%
I will look at markets
in which prices

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.550 align:middle line:90%
are not posted but negotiated.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:54.690 align:middle line:84%
And these are very
interesting markets.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.320 align:middle line:84%
And we have recently
much more data

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:00.800 align:middle line:84%
which allow us to make
some empirical progress

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.280 align:middle line:90%
on these kinds of markets.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.030 align:middle line:84%
So if you think
in many settings,

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:08.700 align:middle line:84%
people negotiate
prices with the seller.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.590 align:middle line:84%
So if you buy a car,
a house, a mortgage,

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.190 align:middle line:84%
so many kinds of consumer
financial markets

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:19.700 align:middle line:84%
have this feature that
prices are somehow negotiated

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.510 align:middle line:90%
and various kinds of services.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:26.750 align:middle line:84%
So if you get a contractor
for your home or, let's say,

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.950 align:middle line:84%
a plumber or
something like that.

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:31.280 align:middle line:84%
So in these markets,
it's oftentimes

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:33.230 align:middle line:84%
particularly hard
for consumers to know

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.230 align:middle line:84%
what is the fair
or the right price,

00:04:36.230 --> 00:04:38.030 align:middle line:84%
because searching is
particularly costly

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:40.380 align:middle line:84%
and you have to go
through some process.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:44.570 align:middle line:84%
And there is oftentimes this
element of idiosyncratic pricing

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.970 align:middle line:84%
that makes that
even more difficult.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.588 align:middle line:84%
What makes this also
very interesting,

00:04:49.588 --> 00:04:51.630 align:middle line:84%
and this is something that
we will explore today,

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:55.260 align:middle line:84%
is that there are potential
distributional implications

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:58.650 align:middle line:84%
of pricing And policies
such as mergers that we

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:01.226 align:middle line:90%
think about as economists.

00:05:01.226 --> 00:05:04.000 align:middle line:90%


00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.070 align:middle line:84%
An empirical
challenge that arises

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:10.780 align:middle line:84%
when looking at those markets
is that if sellers are quoting

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.380 align:middle line:84%
different prices to
buyers, but we only

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:16.240 align:middle line:84%
observe the transaction
prices, this

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.610 align:middle line:90%
is a selected set of prices.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:21.090 align:middle line:84%
And so we need to
know how we can

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:23.340 align:middle line:84%
map this back to the prices
that are actually offered.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.410 align:middle line:90%


00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.620 align:middle line:90%
So here's the roadmap for today.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.700 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk first
about a paper that's

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:37.860 align:middle line:84%
kind of conceptually
very interesting

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.860 align:middle line:84%
and ties back to
the theory lecture

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:44.670 align:middle line:90%
that Glenn held on search.

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.300 align:middle line:84%
And then a more empirically
oriented applications

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:50.160 align:middle line:90%
on mergers and search markets.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.960 align:middle line:84%
And if I have time, I'll
probably not have too much time,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.960 align:middle line:84%
I'm also going to talk
about my job market paper

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.065 align:middle line:84%
for a little bit,
which is in this space.

00:05:59.065 --> 00:06:02.580 align:middle line:90%


00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:08.870 align:middle line:84%
So let me talk about this
paper by Hong and Shum.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:13.070 align:middle line:84%
So it's really an
interesting exercise.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.460 align:middle line:84%
And one thing that
makes it interesting

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:20.570 align:middle line:84%
is it takes really this idea
that was in Stigler's quote

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:21.770 align:middle line:90%
to the extreme.

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:29.000 align:middle line:84%
So it asks what can we learn
from a price distribution?

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:33.410 align:middle line:84%
And it imposes a specific
equilibrium, namely

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:36.470 align:middle line:84%
that firms are playing mixed
strategy, mixed strategy

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:38.150 align:middle line:90%
pricing.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.280 align:middle line:84%
The model that it builds
on is Burdett and Judd.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:43.460 align:middle line:84%
This is conceptually quite
close to the style model

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:45.385 align:middle line:84%
that you had seen
in Glenn's lecture.

00:06:45.385 --> 00:06:48.660 align:middle line:90%


00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:55.100 align:middle line:84%
This data only-- this paper
only uses data on prices.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.500 align:middle line:90%
No quantity data.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:04.100 align:middle line:84%
And so, again, the idea here
is we take these mixed strategy

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.770 align:middle line:84%
pricing equilibria
very, very seriously

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.030 align:middle line:84%
and see how far this
gets us empirically.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:10.940 align:middle line:84%
And at the end, we can
discuss a little bit

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:15.235 align:middle line:84%
whether this is a sensible
way to go about this.

00:07:15.235 --> 00:07:17.758 align:middle line:90%


00:07:17.758 --> 00:07:19.800 align:middle line:84%
The other interesting
thing that this paper does,

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.440 align:middle line:84%
it looks at basically
the same problem

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.660 align:middle line:90%
with two different models.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.580 align:middle line:84%
A sequential and a
non-sequential search model.

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:30.420 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
that empirically, it

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.170 align:middle line:90%
matters which one you use.

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:33.750 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's
a good exercise

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:37.950 align:middle line:84%
to go through, to think a little
bit about you're conceptually

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:39.070 align:middle line:90%
interested in something.

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:44.550 align:middle line:84%
There are different kinds of
models, slight variations that

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:48.270 align:middle line:84%
might allow you to in some
cases recover primitives

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:49.810 align:middle line:90%
better than in other cases.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.260 align:middle line:84%
And so I think in
that, the paper

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:55.970 align:middle line:84%
is quite nice that it helps
you sort through these things.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:08:00.010 align:middle line:90%


00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:08.860 align:middle line:84%
So in this setting, consumers
are taking non-sequent-- so

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.020 align:middle line:84%
in the first version
of the paper,

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:15.710 align:middle line:84%
non-sequential draws from
the price distribution.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:22.260 align:middle line:84%
So they're basically committing
ex ante to l number of draws.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:28.080 align:middle line:84%
And the way they do this is by
minimizing their total cost,

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:29.890 align:middle line:90%
which is their search cost.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:33.000 align:middle line:84%
Each consumer has an
idiosyncratic cost

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.840 align:middle line:84%
for searching that's
constant across searches.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.030 align:middle line:84%
And then there's an expected
price that results from taking l

00:08:42.030 --> 00:08:44.440 align:middle line:84%
draws from the
price distribution.

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:48.440 align:middle line:84%
And one of the
draws is for free.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:52.280 align:middle line:84%
So one thing I
should have mentioned

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.770 align:middle line:84%
is that consumers
in this market,

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:58.640 align:middle line:84%
they have a unit
inelastic demand.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:01.535 align:middle line:84%
That means they purchase one
product for sure, but not more

00:09:01.535 --> 00:09:02.035 align:middle line:90%
than that.

00:09:02.035 --> 00:09:10.070 align:middle line:90%


00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:12.250 align:middle line:84%
And so this here is
the price distribution

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:16.130 align:middle line:84%
that comes out of the mixed
strategy pricing equilibrium.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:24.560 align:middle line:84%
So again, this expression here
is just the expected lowest

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.070 align:middle line:90%
price out of taking l draws.

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:32.770 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about what
consumers are doing here?

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:36.240 align:middle line:84%
OK, so how can we
think about this?

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:41.530 align:middle line:84%
So consumers are comparing the
marginal cost of searching,

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:47.690 align:middle line:84%
which is ci, to the marginal
benefit of searching.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.670 align:middle line:84%
What is the marginal
benefit of searching?

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:54.860 align:middle line:84%
Well, if I think about going
from i to i plus 1 draws,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.510 align:middle line:84%
it's how much lower the price
is if I take an extra draw.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.580 align:middle line:84%
So the marginal
benefit of searching

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.400 align:middle line:84%
is the difference in expected
prices from taking another draw

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:05.850 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:09.374 align:middle line:90%


00:10:09.374 --> 00:10:12.790 align:middle line:84%
And so they're
comparing marginal cost

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.193 align:middle line:90%
and marginal benefit.

00:10:14.193 --> 00:10:15.610 align:middle line:84%
As long as the
marginal benefit is

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:17.735 align:middle line:84%
higher than the marginal
cost, the price reduction,

00:10:17.735 --> 00:10:20.145 align:middle line:84%
they're going to increase
the number of draws by 1.

00:10:20.145 --> 00:10:23.210 align:middle line:90%


00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:26.230 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be helpful to
keep track of these types here,

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:29.720 align:middle line:84%
the delta i's, because
these are consumers.

00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:32.330 align:middle line:84%
Consumers, again, have
heterogeneous search costs

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:37.130 align:middle line:84%
who are just indifferent between
drawing i and i plus 1 times

00:10:37.130 --> 00:10:39.320 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:42.840 align:middle line:84%
So we get this kind of
sequence of marginal benefits.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.630 align:middle line:84%
Now, with this in
mind, there's actually

00:10:44.630 --> 00:10:50.270 align:middle line:84%
a really nice way of
displaying what consumers

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:51.840 align:middle line:90%
are doing in this market.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.540 align:middle line:90%
So this here shows the CDF of--

00:10:56.540 --> 00:10:59.900 align:middle line:84%
sorry, the density of the
search cost distribution.

00:10:59.900 --> 00:11:03.380 align:middle line:84%
And consumers are ordered
in terms of how many draws

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:05.870 align:middle line:84%
they're taking from
the price distribution.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.420 align:middle line:84%
Consumers with a very
high search cost,

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:13.260 align:middle line:84%
with a very high search cost,
they only take their free draw.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.313 align:middle line:84%
And as you lower search
costs that take more and more

00:11:16.313 --> 00:11:17.730 align:middle line:84%
draws from the
price distribution.

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:20.370 align:middle line:84%
And again, these are
the difference types

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:24.598 align:middle line:84%
that segment these different
bins of the distribution

00:11:24.598 --> 00:11:25.265 align:middle line:90%
of search costs.

00:11:25.265 --> 00:11:29.450 align:middle line:90%


00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:35.180 align:middle line:84%
So we get these kind of mass
points of consumers that take

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:39.970 align:middle line:90%
i draws from the distribution.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:44.170 align:middle line:90%
And we call those q tilde.

00:11:44.170 --> 00:11:46.090 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.788 align:middle line:84%
In this direction, search
costs are increasing.

00:11:48.788 --> 00:11:50.830 align:middle line:84%
With lower search costs,
you're taking more draws

00:11:50.830 --> 00:11:54.280 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.000 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's now think
about what firms are doing.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:03.160 align:middle line:84%
So this is, again, based on
a model by Burdett and Judd

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:06.460 align:middle line:90%
from 1983.

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:10.200 align:middle line:90%
Firms are posting prices here.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:17.310 align:middle line:84%
And they're doing so maximizing
the margin times the probability

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:18.695 align:middle line:90%
of selling to a consumer.

00:12:18.695 --> 00:12:22.300 align:middle line:90%


00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.030 align:middle line:84%
These q tildes,
remember, were how many

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:28.010 align:middle line:84%
consumers are taking k draws
from the price distribution.

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:30.590 align:middle line:90%


00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:35.630 align:middle line:84%
So the q tilde k of
them, they take k draws.

00:12:35.630 --> 00:12:39.595 align:middle line:84%
And I'm selling to them if I'm
offering them the lowest price.

00:12:39.595 --> 00:12:44.400 align:middle line:90%


00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:47.850 align:middle line:84%
And so, again, you have to
think of this here as the price

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.300 align:middle line:84%
distribution that comes out
of the mixed strategy pricing

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:52.090 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:55.590 align:middle line:84%
This is a smooth,
atomless distribution

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:58.505 align:middle line:84%
has some support points that
will be important in a second.

00:12:58.505 --> 00:13:02.252 align:middle line:90%


00:13:02.252 --> 00:13:09.300 align:middle line:84%
So how can we get some
traction on this empirically?

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:13.200 align:middle line:84%
The restriction that
we're going to use here

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:18.690 align:middle line:84%
is the fact that firms have to
be indifferent between offering

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:20.850 align:middle line:84%
all the prices that are in
the support of the price

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:24.120 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:28.200 align:middle line:84%
As is the standard condition
in a mixed strategy pricing

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:28.950 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.170 align:middle line:84%
Everything that's
in the support,

00:13:31.170 --> 00:13:35.640 align:middle line:84%
you have to be indifferent
between those strategies.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.060 align:middle line:84%
And so in particular,
firms have to be

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:42.780 align:middle line:84%
indifferent between
offering any price p

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.320 align:middle line:84%
and offering the
highest price and only

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.640 align:middle line:84%
sell to consumers that
are not searching.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:52.640 align:middle line:90%


00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.460 align:middle line:84%
So now let's stare
at this for a second.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:57.110 align:middle line:90%


00:13:57.110 --> 00:13:59.270 align:middle line:84%
So what are the
things that we do know

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:02.950 align:middle line:84%
and what are the things that we
do not know in this equation?

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:06.030 align:middle line:90%
We do know the prices.

00:14:06.030 --> 00:14:09.020 align:middle line:84%
Again, as I told
you earlier, they're

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:12.140 align:middle line:84%
starting from the assumption
that the researcher observes

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:13.585 align:middle line:90%
prices in the market.

00:14:13.585 --> 00:14:16.370 align:middle line:90%


00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:19.520 align:middle line:84%
But we do not know
how many consumers

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:23.060 align:middle line:84%
are searching how many
times, as well as what

00:14:23.060 --> 00:14:25.375 align:middle line:90%
the marginal cost is of firms.

00:14:25.375 --> 00:14:32.596 align:middle line:90%


00:14:32.596 --> 00:14:38.270 align:middle line:90%
So this gives us conditions.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.610 align:middle line:84%
Linear equations, in
fact, that allow us

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:44.360 align:middle line:90%
to figure out what q and r are.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.340 align:middle line:84%
Again, because we
observe prices,

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.300 align:middle line:84%
we can construct this
CDF of prices here.

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:56.830 align:middle line:90%


00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:01.690 align:middle line:84%
So as long as we
observe more prices

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:06.010 align:middle line:84%
than the maximum number
of draws that consumers

00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:10.030 align:middle line:84%
can take in this
market, or we have

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:14.650 align:middle line:84%
to observe more prices
than k plus 1 of that,

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:20.350 align:middle line:84%
we can build moment
conditions or equations

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:25.510 align:middle line:84%
from which we simply back
out these unknown q's and r.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:28.000 align:middle line:84%
So this just amounts
to basically counting

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.170 align:middle line:84%
the number of equations relative
to the number of unknowns.

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:34.640 align:middle line:84%
So these are sets
of linear equations.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:37.120 align:middle line:84%
The way this actually
works in the paper

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:43.030 align:middle line:84%
is that you typically are
overidentified because n minus 1

00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:46.000 align:middle line:84%
is much larger than k,
and so you use, let's say,

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.010 align:middle line:84%
a moment based method or,
in fact, in the paper,

00:15:48.010 --> 00:15:54.220 align:middle line:84%
they use empirical likelihood
to back out these unknowns.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:55.770 align:middle line:90%
Again, so what are the unknowns?

00:15:55.770 --> 00:16:00.360 align:middle line:84%
I have k minus 1 of
these unknown q tildes.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.630 align:middle line:84%
Remember, this was this
partition of the search cost

00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:04.490 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:08.600 align:middle line:84%
And you have the
unobserved marginal cost

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:10.640 align:middle line:84%
of firms, which
is r, and assumed

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:11.935 align:middle line:90%
to be constant across firms.

00:16:11.935 --> 00:16:18.260 align:middle line:90%


00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:20.870 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So just real quick, a
place where Hong and Shum are

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.400 align:middle line:84%
cheating a little
bit is they've got

00:16:22.400 --> 00:16:27.560 align:middle line:84%
that f hat of pi, the
CDF evaluated at pi,

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:30.080 align:middle line:84%
and they're setting
that CDF evaluated

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:33.090 align:middle line:84%
pi in the empirical
CDF evaluated at pi.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:34.490 align:middle line:90%
So it's as if--

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:37.470 align:middle line:84%
it's not the prices are
drawn from a distribution.

00:16:37.470 --> 00:16:40.160 align:middle line:84%
They're assuming
that is the true CDF

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:41.810 align:middle line:90%
at every observed price.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.390 align:middle line:84%
The number of prices
is less than the pi.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:46.940 align:middle line:84%
And that's where their
equations come from.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:49.190 align:middle line:84%
Because at every
observed price, they

00:16:49.190 --> 00:16:53.512 align:middle line:90%
assume they know f [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:53.512 --> 00:16:55.220 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: Yeah,
I'll come back to this

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.030 align:middle line:84%
because if you, if you actually
look at the price distributions,

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:03.000 align:middle line:90%
they look a little bit funny.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:03.960 align:middle line:90%
They have mass points.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:10.339 align:middle line:84%
And so it doesn't look super
consistent with an atomless kind

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:11.970 align:middle line:90%
of smooth price distribution.

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:20.220 align:middle line:90%


00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:27.089 align:middle line:84%
So the bottom line
here is that as long

00:17:27.089 --> 00:17:30.090 align:middle line:84%
as we have more
observed prices than k,

00:17:30.090 --> 00:17:32.650 align:middle line:84%
and they haven't really told
us where k is coming from.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:35.140 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to talk
about this later on.

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:38.700 align:middle line:84%
But as long as n minus 1 is
larger than the maximum number

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.380 align:middle line:84%
of draws that
consumers can take,

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:45.540 align:middle line:84%
you can use these equations
here and simply solve

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:47.680 align:middle line:84%
a system of equations
for these unknowns.

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:51.330 align:middle line:84%
Then again, practically,
this is going to boil down

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:52.540 align:middle line:90%
to a moment condition.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:56.860 align:middle line:90%


00:17:56.860 --> 00:18:02.530 align:middle line:84%
OK, so let's now move to
the sequential search model.

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:09.460 align:middle line:84%
The sequential search
model is using a solution

00:18:09.460 --> 00:18:12.920 align:middle line:84%
that you might have seen from,
let's say, the McCall model.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:16.450 align:middle line:84%
I'm assuming you have seen
the labor search McCall

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.110 align:middle line:90%
model in macro or somewhere.

00:18:18.110 --> 00:18:20.320 align:middle line:84%
But let me remind
you how this works.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:24.100 align:middle line:84%
So instead of committing ex ante
to a certain number of draws

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:29.440 align:middle line:84%
from the price distribution,
you're going to draw a price

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.470 align:middle line:84%
and decide whether
or not to draw again.

00:18:32.470 --> 00:18:36.140 align:middle line:84%
And how do you
make this decision?

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:40.420 align:middle line:84%
Well, the typical solution
to this kind of problem

00:18:40.420 --> 00:18:42.670 align:middle line:90%
is a cut off rule.

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:44.117 align:middle line:90%
So there's a reservation price.

00:18:44.117 --> 00:18:46.450 align:middle line:84%
As long as the prices that
you're drawing from the price

00:18:46.450 --> 00:18:49.120 align:middle line:84%
distribution are higher
than the reservation price,

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:50.870 align:middle line:90%
you keep on searching.

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:56.730 align:middle line:84%
But once it falls
below that, you stop.

00:18:56.730 --> 00:18:59.850 align:middle line:84%
And so now the question
is, how do you actually

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:04.560 align:middle line:90%
set this reservation value?

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:08.298 align:middle line:84%
And so here, again, the
same idea as before.

00:19:08.298 --> 00:19:10.840 align:middle line:84%
You're thinking about what is
the marginal cost of searching.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:14.310 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is constant
and equal to ci.

00:19:14.310 --> 00:19:18.780 align:middle line:84%
And what is the marginal
benefit of searching?

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:24.240 align:middle line:84%
Which is basically the weighted
average of price improvements

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.150 align:middle line:84%
that I could get where z
is my reservation value.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:33.330 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is implicitly a
function of my search cost.

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:37.690 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this weighted
average of price improvements.

00:19:37.690 --> 00:19:40.320 align:middle line:84%
This here is the marginal
benefit of searching

00:19:40.320 --> 00:19:44.300 align:middle line:90%
and ci is the marginal cost.

00:19:44.300 --> 00:19:48.580 align:middle line:84%
So z is set such that these
two are equal to each other.

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:51.440 align:middle line:90%


00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:58.010 align:middle line:84%
Does everybody-- may I ask,
have you seen the McCall model?

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:01.420 align:middle line:90%


00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:02.535 align:middle line:90%
So some of you.

00:20:02.535 --> 00:20:06.450 align:middle line:90%


00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:17.310 align:middle line:84%
So with this
solution, it turns out

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:23.370 align:middle line:84%
that empirically, you can make
much less progress, and in fact,

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.990 align:middle line:84%
not rely on the same
argument as we did before.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:29.350 align:middle line:90%
So why is that the case?

00:20:29.350 --> 00:20:33.940 align:middle line:84%
So first, let's think about
the reservation prices.

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:35.700 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that's
different here

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:37.800 align:middle line:84%
is that beforehand,
we had this sort

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:41.130 align:middle line:84%
of discrete number of things
that consumers could do.

00:20:41.130 --> 00:20:45.630 align:middle line:84%
So they were taking a continuous
draw from the search cost

00:20:45.630 --> 00:20:46.300 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:50.310 align:middle line:84%
But still, in the end, there
are only finitely many things

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:51.420 align:middle line:90%
that they could do.

00:20:51.420 --> 00:20:55.620 align:middle line:84%
So you get these kind of bins
of the search cost distribution.

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:58.800 align:middle line:84%
And we were
identifying these bins.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:05.100 align:middle line:84%
Here what is different is
that the optimal strategy

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:09.250 align:middle line:84%
is a function of ci and
every unique search cost

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:14.062 align:middle line:84%
type has a unique
reservation value strategy.

00:21:14.062 --> 00:21:16.980 align:middle line:84%
Except for those that
potentially bunch at the top.

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:19.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you have a
higher reservation

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:21.960 align:middle line:84%
value, then the
highest possible price

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:24.540 align:middle line:84%
in the support of the price
distribution, then of course,

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:28.220 align:middle line:84%
you stop searching when you
reach-- when you drop p bar.

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:31.460 align:middle line:90%


00:21:31.460 --> 00:21:34.340 align:middle line:84%
So now let's think about
the demand of firms

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:36.253 align:middle line:90%
with this kind of strategy.

00:21:36.253 --> 00:21:39.414 align:middle line:90%


00:21:39.414 --> 00:21:44.010 align:middle line:84%
So as before, we can construct
indifference conditions.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:46.740 align:middle line:90%
So what is the demand of a firm?

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:48.990 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to sell
only to consumers

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.527 align:middle line:84%
that have a reservation
value higher than the price p

00:21:51.527 --> 00:21:52.235 align:middle line:90%
that I'm setting.

00:21:52.235 --> 00:21:57.474 align:middle line:90%


00:21:57.474 --> 00:22:00.020 align:middle line:84%
So the demand is
proportional to the mass

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:07.350 align:middle line:84%
of consumers above the price p
that I include, that I charge.

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:11.270 align:middle line:84%
So now again, we can
try and construct

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:17.970 align:middle line:84%
these indifference conditions
for any given price pi.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:21.500 align:middle line:84%
Firms need to be indifferent
between charging that price,

00:22:21.500 --> 00:22:24.590 align:middle line:84%
reaching consumers only
above that have a reservation

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:31.670 align:middle line:84%
value above that price and
the maximum possible price

00:22:31.670 --> 00:22:34.020 align:middle line:84%
in the support of the
price distribution.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:36.820 align:middle line:90%


00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:39.510 align:middle line:84%
Can you see what
the issue here is?

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:42.140 align:middle line:90%


00:22:42.140 --> 00:22:46.940 align:middle line:84%
So the problem is we have a
incidental parameter problem.

00:22:46.940 --> 00:22:53.850 align:middle line:84%
For any nu of those indifference
conditions that are introduced,

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm also introducing
a nu unknown,

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:58.915 align:middle line:84%
which is the mass of
consumers above that point.

00:22:58.915 --> 00:23:01.934 align:middle line:90%


00:23:01.934 --> 00:23:09.080 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, this simple
moment counting versus unknowns

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:13.550 align:middle line:84%
no longer works because
I have n plus 1 unknowns.

00:23:13.550 --> 00:23:17.630 align:middle line:84%
The mass of consumers
below pi and also

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:19.910 align:middle line:90%
the unknown cost of the firm.

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:23.630 align:middle line:84%
But I have n minus 1
indifference conditions.

00:23:23.630 --> 00:23:29.540 align:middle line:84%
So basically we can no
longer use this argument.

00:23:29.540 --> 00:23:32.630 align:middle line:84%
So in the paper, they say to
non-parametrically identify

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:35.840 align:middle line:84%
the search cost distribution,
in the previous case,

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.240 align:middle line:84%
we also didn't quite
non-parametrically identify

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.640 align:middle line:84%
the search cost distribution
because we only got

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:46.160 align:middle line:90%
this partition into these bins.

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:49.820 align:middle line:84%
But here we basically
with this strategy

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:52.040 align:middle line:84%
can learn anything about
the search cost distribution

00:23:52.040 --> 00:23:56.270 align:middle line:84%
unless we're willing to
impose some parametric form.

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:58.470 align:middle line:84%
And so that's what they're
going to do in the end.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:24:02.520 align:middle line:84%
They basically assume that
the search cost distribution

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:05.740 align:middle line:84%
takes on a specific
parametric distribution

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:08.555 align:middle line:84%
and then simply estimate
it based off of that.

00:24:08.555 --> 00:24:14.280 align:middle line:90%


00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:17.452 align:middle line:84%
So this comes back
to Glenn's point.

00:24:17.452 --> 00:24:18.660 align:middle line:90%
What is the application here?

00:24:18.660 --> 00:24:23.820 align:middle line:84%
The application is perhaps not
the most inspiring, but maybe

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:29.580 align:middle line:84%
in this crowd, which is the
different sets of econ graduate

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:30.520 align:middle line:90%
textbooks.

00:24:30.520 --> 00:24:34.230 align:middle line:84%
So Stokey-Lucas,
Lazear, Billingsley--

00:24:34.230 --> 00:24:35.500 align:middle line:90%
Probability and Measure.

00:24:35.500 --> 00:24:38.400 align:middle line:84%
So if you stare at these
price distributions,

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.910 align:middle line:84%
you can ask yourself whether
you think it's plausible

00:24:41.910 --> 00:24:45.390 align:middle line:84%
that these are coming out of
this mixed strategy pricing

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:46.090 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:49.020 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that I, for
example, find somewhat curious

00:24:49.020 --> 00:24:53.250 align:middle line:84%
is that you get these very
large mass points here.

00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:59.800 align:middle line:84%
And remember from Stahl but
also from Burdett and Judd,

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:03.215 align:middle line:84%
we would expect an atomless
smooth distribution of prices.

00:25:03.215 --> 00:25:10.510 align:middle line:90%


00:25:10.510 --> 00:25:12.123 align:middle line:84%
Let me show you
some of the results.

00:25:12.123 --> 00:25:14.750 align:middle line:90%


00:25:14.750 --> 00:25:18.930 align:middle line:84%
So first, they have to
figure out what k is.

00:25:18.930 --> 00:25:20.870 align:middle line:84%
Remember, k was
the number of draws

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:23.360 align:middle line:84%
from the maximum number of
draws that consumers take

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:24.980 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:27.810 align:middle line:84%
The way they do this
is somewhat hacky.

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:31.670 align:middle line:84%
They basically just look
at what is the highest

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:34.730 align:middle line:90%
k for which my model converges.

00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:38.150 align:middle line:90%
So estimate the model with that.

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:40.400 align:middle line:84%
And so one striking
thing that they find

00:25:40.400 --> 00:25:47.000 align:middle line:84%
is that for most of
these different markets,

00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:50.150 align:middle line:84%
the average consumer
only searches once.

00:25:50.150 --> 00:25:52.940 align:middle line:84%
So about 50% of consumers
only take one draw

00:25:52.940 --> 00:25:55.190 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:59.870 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, only for one
of the textbooks, Lazear,

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:04.490 align:middle line:84%
you actually have consumers that
take three draws from the price

00:26:04.490 --> 00:26:07.310 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:14.530 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about this
here, one potential issue

00:26:14.530 --> 00:26:18.070 align:middle line:84%
with having 50%
of consumers only

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:22.030 align:middle line:84%
searching once, which
remember you get these cut off

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:23.650 align:middle line:90%
points for consumers.

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.440 align:middle line:84%
Let me just go back
to this briefly

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:28.270 align:middle line:90%
to illustrate this point.

00:26:28.270 --> 00:26:35.120 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm identifying
here are these partitions.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:39.110 align:middle line:84%
So I'm getting out of the model
a partition of the search cost

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:40.180 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:44.810 align:middle line:84%
Now, if let's say delta
1 lies very, very low

00:26:44.810 --> 00:26:48.440 align:middle line:84%
and most consumers
only search once,

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:51.890 align:middle line:84%
then I don't actually
learn all that much

00:26:51.890 --> 00:26:53.870 align:middle line:84%
about the price distribution
above that point--

00:26:53.870 --> 00:26:56.037 align:middle line:84%
about the search cost
distribution above that point.

00:26:56.037 --> 00:27:00.380 align:middle line:90%


00:27:00.380 --> 00:27:05.840 align:middle line:84%
So the problem is that what
we're really recovering here

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:12.080 align:middle line:84%
is not a primitive but an
endogenous object, which

00:27:12.080 --> 00:27:16.060 align:middle line:84%
is the search
strategy of consumers.

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:18.970 align:middle line:84%
And so we have
to, in some sense,

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:22.660 align:middle line:84%
cross our fingers and hope that
how consumers are searching

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:25.870 align:middle line:84%
in the market tells us
enough about the search cost

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:26.750 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:30.860 align:middle line:84%
But it could just be that most
consumers don't search at all.

00:27:30.860 --> 00:27:33.520 align:middle line:84%
And then I don't
really learn anything.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:37.600 align:middle line:84%
So that's kind of one potential
shortcoming of this approach

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:41.890 align:middle line:84%
is that this identification
argument doesn't directly

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:44.410 align:middle line:84%
map to the distribution,
but it maps

00:27:44.410 --> 00:27:47.410 align:middle line:84%
to what consumers are
doing in the market.

00:27:47.410 --> 00:27:51.460 align:middle line:84%
If you think about this paper
and let me comment a little bit

00:27:51.460 --> 00:27:58.480 align:middle line:84%
on this, if you think about the
exercise, just using price data

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:03.490 align:middle line:90%
is a somewhat heroic exercise.

00:28:03.490 --> 00:28:05.830 align:middle line:84%
And you have to think
about this as being this

00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:08.320 align:middle line:84%
was one of the very first
empirical papers on search

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:11.680 align:middle line:84%
or papers that use the
equilibrium conditions that you

00:28:11.680 --> 00:28:14.620 align:middle line:84%
get out of a search model to
say something empirically.

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:17.380 align:middle line:90%


00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:19.240 align:middle line:84%
So one thing you may
want to ask yourself

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:23.500 align:middle line:84%
is how plausible you find
it that firms are really

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:25.305 align:middle line:90%
setting prices by mixing.

00:28:25.305 --> 00:28:29.773 align:middle line:90%


00:28:29.773 --> 00:28:31.690 align:middle line:84%
And whether there are
alternative explanations

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:32.857 align:middle line:90%
why we see different prices.

00:28:32.857 --> 00:28:36.670 align:middle line:90%


00:28:36.670 --> 00:28:39.410 align:middle line:84%
This wouldn't be on the top
of my list of complaints,

00:28:39.410 --> 00:28:43.730 align:middle line:84%
but the marginal cost of firms
are assumed to be constant here.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.570 align:middle line:84%
So you could take
some issue with that.

00:28:46.570 --> 00:28:49.870 align:middle line:84%
And then I want to
point out something

00:28:49.870 --> 00:28:54.430 align:middle line:84%
that is more of a common
critique of many papers

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:56.680 align:middle line:90%
in the search literature.

00:28:56.680 --> 00:29:02.230 align:middle line:84%
So first, most papers find
very large search costs.

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:08.620 align:middle line:84%
And one issue is that you look
at the dispersion of prices

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:11.170 align:middle line:84%
and you say that all
of this dispersion, all

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:13.420 align:middle line:84%
those differences
across prices is really

00:29:13.420 --> 00:29:15.790 align:middle line:84%
kind of structural
variation in a sense

00:29:15.790 --> 00:29:20.680 align:middle line:84%
that all of that variation has
an economic interpretation.

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:23.260 align:middle line:84%
As opposed to things
that happen with data,

00:29:23.260 --> 00:29:25.660 align:middle line:84%
it could be that there's,
let's say, measurement error

00:29:25.660 --> 00:29:30.800 align:middle line:84%
in prices or other reasons why
the prices that you observe

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.520 align:middle line:84%
are not exactly
equal to each other.

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:37.460 align:middle line:84%
And so in these models,
you load typically

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.880 align:middle line:84%
almost the entire
variation of prices

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:44.660 align:middle line:84%
onto this search
cost distribution.

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:50.090 align:middle line:84%
And so that's part of the reason
why in many of these papers,

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:53.180 align:middle line:84%
you find somewhat excessively
large search costs.

00:29:53.180 --> 00:29:57.352 align:middle line:84%
And so I think that's something
that this literature has

00:29:57.352 --> 00:29:58.560 align:middle line:90%
to grapple with a little bit.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:01.190 align:middle line:84%
But it's actually common
to other literatures that

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:03.710 align:middle line:84%
interpret all price
variation as structural,

00:30:03.710 --> 00:30:06.640 align:middle line:84%
which is, for example,
also true in auctions.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:09.610 align:middle line:84%
So the other thing that
you could potentially

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:13.600 align:middle line:84%
complain about here is that
these sellers of textbooks, they

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:16.090 align:middle line:84%
might be differentiated
in other ways.

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:17.680 align:middle line:84%
Some of them might
have funny names

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:21.160 align:middle line:84%
or located in places that
people, I don't know,

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:22.160 align:middle line:90%
don't like that much.

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:25.090 align:middle line:84%
And so for that reason, they
have to charge different prices

00:30:25.090 --> 00:30:26.560 align:middle line:90%
for the same kind of product.

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:29.062 align:middle line:84%
So although these are in
principle very homogeneous

00:30:29.062 --> 00:30:30.520 align:middle line:84%
products, you could
think there are

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:34.360 align:middle line:84%
other unobserved
differentiating factors that

00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:35.940 align:middle line:90%
lead to differences in prices.

00:30:35.940 --> 00:30:40.350 align:middle line:90%


00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:44.350 align:middle line:84%
So again, this is the way you
have to think about this paper.

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:46.380 align:middle line:84%
This is, I would
say, mostly kind

00:30:46.380 --> 00:30:48.220 align:middle line:84%
of a conceptually
interesting exercise.

00:30:48.220 --> 00:30:50.520 align:middle line:84%
It teaches us something
about using these equilibrium

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:51.750 align:middle line:90%
restrictions.

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:58.860 align:middle line:84%
It's perhaps not a paper
that you would use nowadays

00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:01.230 align:middle line:84%
to really think
about such frictions,

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.480 align:middle line:84%
in part because of this
very strong equilibrium

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:07.140 align:middle line:84%
restriction that firms are
playing mixed strategies, which

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:11.070 align:middle line:84%
in some settings we
know is sensible.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:16.050 align:middle line:84%
Let's think about, for instance,
sports and other places

00:31:16.050 --> 00:31:18.010 align:middle line:84%
where we know that
mixing makes sense.

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:20.920 align:middle line:84%
But here, you can do
some introspection.

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:22.420 align:middle line:84%
I find it somewhat
less plausible.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:25.680 align:middle line:90%


00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.500 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going
to talk about next

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:34.980 align:middle line:84%
is a paper that is much
more empirically motivated

00:31:34.980 --> 00:31:38.710 align:middle line:84%
by Jason Allen, Rob Clarke,
and Jean-Francois Houde.

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:42.420 align:middle line:90%
It came out in the AR in 2013.

00:31:42.420 --> 00:31:47.850 align:middle line:84%
And it basically asks,
what are the implications

00:31:47.850 --> 00:31:53.520 align:middle line:84%
of a merger in a market
where prices are negotiated?

00:31:53.520 --> 00:31:59.820 align:middle line:84%
So just to remind you
a little bit, so in IO,

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:01.950 align:middle line:84%
we have a very
developed machinery

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:05.910 align:middle line:84%
for thinking about mergers
in posted price markets.

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:08.230 align:middle line:90%
So we estimate demand.

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:11.280 align:middle line:84%
We have some ownership
matrix with demand.

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:15.600 align:middle line:84%
And the ownership matrix we can
compute first order conditions

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.090 align:middle line:90%
and can recover costs.

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:18.840 align:middle line:84%
And once we have costs,
we can, let's say,

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:24.900 align:middle line:84%
change the ownership matrix
and recompute prices.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.377 align:middle line:84%
Up until very recently,
there was not a lot of work

00:32:28.377 --> 00:32:30.960 align:middle line:84%
that thinks about what are some
of the implications of mergers

00:32:30.960 --> 00:32:33.377 align:middle line:84%
in markets where different
consumers pay different prices.

00:32:33.377 --> 00:32:38.300 align:middle line:90%


00:32:38.300 --> 00:32:43.590 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to look at this
in the Canadian mortgage market.

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:47.820 align:middle line:84%
They have some great data on
insured mortgage contracts,

00:32:47.820 --> 00:32:52.440 align:middle line:84%
which are collected
directly from the insurer.

00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:55.440 align:middle line:84%
And what is nice about the fact
that these mortgages are insured

00:32:55.440 --> 00:33:01.050 align:middle line:84%
is that they can claim that
these contracts are very, very

00:33:01.050 --> 00:33:03.990 align:middle line:84%
homogeneous, that
consumer risk is not

00:33:03.990 --> 00:33:05.460 align:middle line:84%
playing a large
role in explaining

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:06.990 align:middle line:90%
price differentiation.

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:11.010 align:middle line:84%
So they claim that this is a
very, very homogeneous product

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.690 align:middle line:84%
because idiosyncratic
risk here is insured away.

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:17.890 align:middle line:90%


00:33:17.890 --> 00:33:21.820 align:middle line:84%
So part of why I
like this paper is it

00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:26.110 align:middle line:84%
provides a very nice combination
of treatment effect analysis

00:33:26.110 --> 00:33:29.230 align:middle line:84%
of an actual merger that
happened in the data

00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:37.690 align:middle line:84%
and interpreting this variation
through a structural model.

00:33:37.690 --> 00:33:40.330 align:middle line:84%
And so I'll try to
make this clear today,

00:33:40.330 --> 00:33:42.190 align:middle line:84%
but there's basically
a very nice connection

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:44.170 align:middle line:84%
between the treatment
effect analysis

00:33:44.170 --> 00:33:47.934 align:middle line:90%
and the structural analysis.

00:33:47.934 --> 00:33:51.630 align:middle line:84%
So there's a merger between
two large lenders that

00:33:51.630 --> 00:33:55.800 align:middle line:84%
happens in the data,
which has implications

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:57.730 align:middle line:90%
for different markets.

00:33:57.730 --> 00:34:02.060 align:middle line:84%
In some markets, you have
branches from both lenders.

00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:05.560 align:middle line:84%
In some other markets, none
of the lenders have branches.

00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:08.205 align:middle line:84%
And then that changes the
choice set of consumers

00:34:08.205 --> 00:34:09.455 align:middle line:90%
differentially across markets.

00:34:09.455 --> 00:34:14.540 align:middle line:90%


00:34:14.540 --> 00:34:19.159 align:middle line:84%
So here's a picture of what
happened in this market

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:21.159 align:middle line:90%
over time.

00:34:21.159 --> 00:34:26.000 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that's
pretty clear and visible

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:29.120 align:middle line:84%
is that over time, this
market has become much, much

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:31.400 align:middle line:90%
more consolidated.

00:34:31.400 --> 00:34:36.330 align:middle line:84%
So initially, there were banks
and credit unions, trust shares.

00:34:36.330 --> 00:34:41.330 align:middle line:84%
So these are basically lenders
that are owned by their members.

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:43.830 align:middle line:84%
And then there is what they
refer to as the big eight.

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:49.760 align:middle line:84%
These are the largest banks
such as TD Bank, Nova Scotia,

00:34:49.760 --> 00:34:52.489 align:middle line:90%
The Royal Bank and others.

00:34:52.489 --> 00:34:56.540 align:middle line:84%
And these have been
subsequently overtaking trusts.

00:34:56.540 --> 00:34:58.910 align:middle line:84%
There were also some
regulatory changes,

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:02.815 align:middle line:84%
and so the market has
consolidated over time.

00:35:02.815 --> 00:35:08.770 align:middle line:90%


00:35:08.770 --> 00:35:13.840 align:middle line:84%
So some institutional details
that are going to be relevant.

00:35:13.840 --> 00:35:17.440 align:middle line:84%
So consumers here
have to go to a lender

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:22.080 align:middle line:84%
to figure out what the
price of their mortgage is.

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:25.420 align:middle line:84%
So they have to talk to
the local branch managers.

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:28.060 align:middle line:84%
And the local branches,
they're posting prices,

00:35:28.060 --> 00:35:34.130 align:middle line:84%
but the consumers can negotiate
discounts from those prices.

00:35:34.130 --> 00:35:36.140 align:middle line:84%
And they have
explicit incentives

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:41.000 align:middle line:84%
to not give too many discounts,
because their compensation

00:35:41.000 --> 00:35:43.220 align:middle line:84%
is affected by the
level of discounts.

00:35:43.220 --> 00:35:47.270 align:middle line:84%
And as they claim in
the paper, they also

00:35:47.270 --> 00:35:51.767 align:middle line:84%
are explicitly told not to
compete with other branches that

00:35:51.767 --> 00:35:52.975 align:middle line:90%
are owned by the same lender.

00:35:52.975 --> 00:35:59.596 align:middle line:90%


00:35:59.596 --> 00:36:08.640 align:middle line:84%
So because the data here has
essentially administrative data

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.620 align:middle line:84%
that comes from
these insurers, they

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:14.340 align:middle line:84%
see very, very rich information
about both the consumers

00:36:14.340 --> 00:36:16.650 align:middle line:84%
and the type of
contract that they get.

00:36:16.650 --> 00:36:20.490 align:middle line:84%
So they see, for instance,
the consumer's home location,

00:36:20.490 --> 00:36:26.850 align:middle line:84%
the loan size, house price,
things like loan to value,

00:36:26.850 --> 00:36:30.392 align:middle line:84%
and demographics such
as income and even

00:36:30.392 --> 00:36:32.975 align:middle line:84%
whether or not they had a prior
relationship with this lender.

00:36:32.975 --> 00:36:36.932 align:middle line:90%


00:36:36.932 --> 00:36:38.390 align:middle line:84%
Another nice thing
that they can do

00:36:38.390 --> 00:36:40.432 align:middle line:84%
is because they see the
consumer's home location,

00:36:40.432 --> 00:36:46.320 align:middle line:84%
they can actually define markets
around consumer's home address.

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:50.840 align:middle line:84%
And that's in terms of market
definition usually pretty good,

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:53.480 align:middle line:84%
because then you don't get
these weird cases where somebody

00:36:53.480 --> 00:36:57.680 align:middle line:84%
lives at the border of a
market and would potentially

00:36:57.680 --> 00:37:00.590 align:middle line:84%
shop from a market
that's defined

00:37:00.590 --> 00:37:03.140 align:middle line:90%
as a separate one in your data.

00:37:03.140 --> 00:37:05.788 align:middle line:84%
And so because the Canadians
do this in kilometers,

00:37:05.788 --> 00:37:06.955 align:middle line:90%
so of course, most sensible.

00:37:06.955 --> 00:37:10.090 align:middle line:90%


00:37:10.090 --> 00:37:13.490 align:middle line:84%
So what are the treatment
and control groups here?

00:37:13.490 --> 00:37:15.970 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to only
focus on the short run

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:17.650 align:middle line:90%
effect of the merger.

00:37:17.650 --> 00:37:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So that means that they're not
going to look at entry and exit,

00:37:21.970 --> 00:37:25.300 align:middle line:84%
but only at the immediate
change in prices

00:37:25.300 --> 00:37:29.220 align:middle line:84%
that the year after
the merger happened.

00:37:29.220 --> 00:37:32.370 align:middle line:84%
And so I'm only talking about
the merger here because I cannot

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:35.550 align:middle line:84%
actually disclose who the
lenders were that merged.

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:39.830 align:middle line:84%
So these were two
larger lenders.

00:37:39.830 --> 00:37:45.260 align:middle line:84%
And so what are what
are the control markets?

00:37:45.260 --> 00:37:49.640 align:middle line:84%
There are some markets in
which neither of the lenders

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:51.620 align:middle line:84%
or only one of
them were present.

00:37:51.620 --> 00:37:52.892 align:middle line:90%
These are the control markets.

00:37:52.892 --> 00:37:54.350 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
markets in which

00:37:54.350 --> 00:37:59.000 align:middle line:84%
both of the merging
lenders had a branch.

00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:02.510 align:middle line:84%
And in those markets,
competition is now reduced.

00:38:02.510 --> 00:38:06.110 align:middle line:84%
Because, again,
according to this story

00:38:06.110 --> 00:38:08.240 align:middle line:84%
that lenders are
internalizing and not

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:11.370 align:middle line:84%
competing against branches
in their own network.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.640 align:middle line:84%
The choice set has
now been reduced.

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:16.580 align:middle line:84%
So consumers cannot compare
as many prices as before.

00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:21.210 align:middle line:84%
Any questions about the
institutional details here?

00:38:21.210 --> 00:38:23.200 align:middle line:90%
So here's what I do.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:25.350 align:middle line:84%
I first have a
descriptive exercise

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:30.510 align:middle line:84%
where they look at
the price variation

00:38:30.510 --> 00:38:32.083 align:middle line:90%
that they observe in the data.

00:38:32.083 --> 00:38:34.500 align:middle line:84%
And again, what's important
here is that they can credibly

00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.710 align:middle line:84%
claim that most of that
is not because of risk

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:41.370 align:middle line:90%
variation across consumers.

00:38:41.370 --> 00:38:47.550 align:middle line:84%
So they first run a
regression of the margin

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:51.330 align:middle line:84%
of a consumer, that's the
rate that they observe

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:56.500 align:middle line:84%
in the data minus the bond rate,
on a set of control variables.

00:38:56.500 --> 00:38:59.340 align:middle line:84%
So how long the
mortgage was, how large

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:01.090 align:middle line:90%
the size of the mortgage was.

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So the idea here
of this regression

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.430 align:middle line:84%
is that you really want
to get at the unobserved

00:39:05.430 --> 00:39:07.270 align:middle line:90%
component of the price.

00:39:07.270 --> 00:39:09.930 align:middle line:84%
So there could still be
differences across consumers,

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:13.590 align:middle line:84%
because again, one consumer gets
a larger mortgage than another.

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:16.090 align:middle line:84%
Or there could be
differences in FICO scores.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:18.990 align:middle line:84%
So there could be some sort
of perceived residual risk

00:39:18.990 --> 00:39:20.080 align:middle line:90%
of a consumer.

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:22.620 align:middle line:84%
But they want to look
at once we control

00:39:22.620 --> 00:39:24.960 align:middle line:84%
for all these things, how
much price variation is

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:26.580 align:middle line:90%
left after that?

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:28.350 align:middle line:84%
And so the key
object of interest

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:30.910 align:middle line:84%
here is this what they
call negotiated margin.

00:39:30.910 --> 00:39:32.710 align:middle line:84%
It's the residual
from this regression.

00:39:32.710 --> 00:39:37.020 align:middle line:90%


00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:40.410 align:middle line:84%
So this is what
they mostly analyze.

00:39:40.410 --> 00:39:44.010 align:middle line:84%
So again, control variables here
also account for time variation

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:44.770 align:middle line:90%
and so on.

00:39:44.770 --> 00:39:50.020 align:middle line:90%


00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:52.550 align:middle line:90%
So here are some summary tables.

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:55.990 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot going on, but I
want to point out a few things.

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:59.830 align:middle line:84%
So up here we have
control markets.

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:04.330 align:middle line:84%
And down here we have
treatment markets.

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:07.300 align:middle line:90%
And so both before and after.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:12.010 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that's true
is that the control markets

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:14.710 align:middle line:84%
have somewhat different
consumers than treatment

00:40:14.710 --> 00:40:15.620 align:middle line:90%
markets.

00:40:15.620 --> 00:40:17.620 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, in
the control markets,

00:40:17.620 --> 00:40:19.990 align:middle line:84%
consumers' house value
is somewhat lower.

00:40:19.990 --> 00:40:22.150 align:middle line:84%
Consumers also have
somewhat lower income

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:23.830 align:middle line:90%
than in the treatment markets.

00:40:23.830 --> 00:40:26.000 align:middle line:84%
But on other variables,
they're fairly balanced.

00:40:26.000 --> 00:40:30.490 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, loan to
value is almost identical.

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:33.550 align:middle line:90%
FICO scores are quite similar.

00:40:33.550 --> 00:40:36.640 align:middle line:84%
The number of renters is
pretty similar and so on.

00:40:36.640 --> 00:40:40.850 align:middle line:84%
So they appear to be quite
balanced on observables.

00:40:40.850 --> 00:40:44.210 align:middle line:90%


00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:46.100 align:middle line:84%
Again, the key
object of interest

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:49.310 align:middle line:90%
is this negotiated margin here.

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:52.700 align:middle line:84%
And the initial
negotiated margin

00:40:52.700 --> 00:40:55.670 align:middle line:84%
is very similar
across both markets.

00:40:55.670 --> 00:40:56.600 align:middle line:90%
So this is here.

00:40:56.600 --> 00:40:59.060 align:middle line:84%
Again, control and treatment
in the before period.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:02.970 align:middle line:84%
And it's 106 and 107
basis points respectively.

00:41:02.970 --> 00:41:06.430 align:middle line:90%


00:41:06.430 --> 00:41:11.560 align:middle line:84%
So now we can look at this
negotiated margin in the after

00:41:11.560 --> 00:41:12.860 align:middle line:90%
period.

00:41:12.860 --> 00:41:16.680 align:middle line:84%
And in both markets,
it goes up to 167

00:41:16.680 --> 00:41:19.720 align:middle line:84%
in the control
markets and a bit more

00:41:19.720 --> 00:41:29.040 align:middle line:84%
than that to 172 in
the treatment markets,

00:41:29.040 --> 00:41:33.022 align:middle line:84%
where both lenders, both
merging lenders had a branch,

00:41:33.022 --> 00:41:34.730 align:middle line:84%
and therefore the
choice set was reduced.

00:41:34.730 --> 00:41:39.000 align:middle line:90%


00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:41.270 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that
is interesting,

00:41:41.270 --> 00:41:44.510 align:middle line:84%
you see it directly in the
summary statistics here

00:41:44.510 --> 00:41:49.223 align:middle line:84%
is the following, which is
that if you look at this

00:41:49.223 --> 00:41:51.140 align:middle line:84%
was the average effect
that I just pointed out

00:41:51.140 --> 00:41:52.320 align:middle line:90%
in the negotiated margin.

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:56.420 align:middle line:84%
But if you look at across
the distribution of prices,

00:41:56.420 --> 00:41:59.990 align:middle line:84%
you see that the 75th percentile
of the negotiated margin is

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:01.980 align:middle line:84%
again very, very
close to each other.

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.160 align:middle line:84%
They're very close
in the after period.

00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:09.050 align:middle line:84%
But there's larger difference,
about eight basis points,

00:42:09.050 --> 00:42:12.630 align:middle line:90%
in the 25th percentile.

00:42:12.630 --> 00:42:18.480 align:middle line:84%
So it appears that most of
this effect of higher average

00:42:18.480 --> 00:42:20.340 align:middle line:84%
negotiated margin is
coming from the fact

00:42:20.340 --> 00:42:23.337 align:middle line:84%
that consumers at the low
end of the price distribution

00:42:23.337 --> 00:42:24.545 align:middle line:90%
are now paying higher prices.

00:42:24.545 --> 00:42:27.290 align:middle line:90%


00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:30.450 align:middle line:84%
And so this is kind of a theme
that will run through this.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:33.112 align:middle line:84%
I told you at the
beginning that what

00:42:33.112 --> 00:42:34.820 align:middle line:84%
makes this paper
interesting is that they

00:42:34.820 --> 00:42:36.620 align:middle line:84%
want to look at the
distributional effects

00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:37.410 align:middle line:90%
of mergers.

00:42:37.410 --> 00:42:41.900 align:middle line:84%
And it's tying this back to
the theme of this class, which

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:44.010 align:middle line:90%
are search costs.

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:48.950 align:middle line:84%
The idea is that different
consumers search differently

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:50.790 align:middle line:84%
as a function of
their search costs.

00:42:50.790 --> 00:42:53.060 align:middle line:84%
And what this implies for
their search strategy,

00:42:53.060 --> 00:42:55.670 align:middle line:84%
they get different
kinds of prices.

00:42:55.670 --> 00:42:58.490 align:middle line:84%
And if I'm a consumer who
basically never searches,

00:42:58.490 --> 00:43:00.890 align:middle line:84%
then the merger
will probably not

00:43:00.890 --> 00:43:03.410 align:middle line:90%
have as big of an effect on me.

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.180 align:middle line:84%
If I'm a consumer
that searches a lot,

00:43:05.180 --> 00:43:10.160 align:middle line:84%
then having an extra
lender that I can go to

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:12.840 align:middle line:84%
is going to potentially
affect my price.

00:43:12.840 --> 00:43:15.680 align:middle line:84%
And so what is nice
that is this idea

00:43:15.680 --> 00:43:19.626 align:middle line:84%
is already visible here directly
in the summary statistics.

00:43:19.626 --> 00:43:27.320 align:middle line:90%


00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:29.240 align:middle line:84%
So this would normally
be now a great table

00:43:29.240 --> 00:43:33.230 align:middle line:84%
to ask my 14.03
undergrads to compute

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:37.230 align:middle line:84%
the difference in difference
treatment effect from this.

00:43:37.230 --> 00:43:40.160 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to
just show it to you.

00:43:40.160 --> 00:43:44.270 align:middle line:90%
So it's not very large.

00:43:44.270 --> 00:43:47.660 align:middle line:84%
Depending on how you look at it,
whether you account for trends

00:43:47.660 --> 00:43:52.040 align:middle line:84%
or not, you can do this either
with a matching estimator

00:43:52.040 --> 00:43:54.570 align:middle line:84%
or not, but the results
are fairly consistent.

00:43:54.570 --> 00:43:55.840 align:middle line:90%
It's about seven basis points.

00:43:55.840 --> 00:43:58.620 align:middle line:90%


00:43:58.620 --> 00:44:01.620 align:middle line:84%
And so I think in
the paper, they

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:07.200 align:middle line:84%
say for the average
mortgage in this data,

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:12.930 align:middle line:84%
this implies about an additional
$5 of extra payments per month.

00:44:12.930 --> 00:44:16.390 align:middle line:84%
So the average
effect is not large.

00:44:16.390 --> 00:44:21.780 align:middle line:84%
And it suggests that this
is a fairly, to begin with,

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:23.000 align:middle line:90%
a fairly competitive market.

00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:25.640 align:middle line:90%


00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:34.380 align:middle line:84%
But what it tells us is that
before IO people started

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:37.140 align:middle line:84%
looking at these markets,
one view of the world

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:39.120 align:middle line:84%
could be that most of
the variation that we

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:42.355 align:middle line:84%
see in these kinds of markets is
due to risk or cost differences.

00:44:42.355 --> 00:44:43.980 align:middle line:84%
And what this shows
is that potentially

00:44:43.980 --> 00:44:45.995 align:middle line:84%
there's a market power
explanation here.

00:44:45.995 --> 00:44:49.680 align:middle line:90%


00:44:49.680 --> 00:44:52.450 align:middle line:90%
I think I said this.

00:44:52.450 --> 00:44:54.270 align:middle line:84%
And again, for people
who search a lot,

00:44:54.270 --> 00:44:58.290 align:middle line:84%
you would expect this to be
a fairly competitive market.

00:44:58.290 --> 00:45:00.990 align:middle line:84%
So this was the average
treatment effect.

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:04.680 align:middle line:84%
So next we're going to look
at the treatment effects

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:07.980 align:middle line:84%
at different points of
the price distribution.

00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:15.510 align:middle line:84%
So for that I have to introduce
some additional notation here.

00:45:15.510 --> 00:45:18.630 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're interested
in the distribution

00:45:18.630 --> 00:45:21.900 align:middle line:90%
of negotiated margins.

00:45:21.900 --> 00:45:24.660 align:middle line:84%
And they're going to index
those here with g and t,

00:45:24.660 --> 00:45:26.790 align:middle line:84%
where g stands
for whether or not

00:45:26.790 --> 00:45:30.900 align:middle line:84%
you're part of the treatment
or the control market.

00:45:30.900 --> 00:45:35.010 align:middle line:84%
And t stands for whether or
not it was before or after

00:45:35.010 --> 00:45:36.035 align:middle line:90%
the merger happened.

00:45:36.035 --> 00:45:40.330 align:middle line:90%


00:45:40.330 --> 00:45:44.320 align:middle line:84%
What we want to
make this comparison

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:48.820 align:middle line:84%
is a counterfactual distribution
of negotiated margins

00:45:48.820 --> 00:45:50.830 align:middle line:90%
for the treated markets.

00:45:50.830 --> 00:45:53.725 align:middle line:84%
So we would like to know
what, just as you would

00:45:53.725 --> 00:45:54.850 align:middle line:90%
in your typical difference.

00:45:54.850 --> 00:45:56.892 align:middle line:84%
In difference analysis,
you make some assumptions

00:45:56.892 --> 00:45:58.960 align:middle line:84%
to know what would the
counterfactual market have

00:45:58.960 --> 00:45:59.820 align:middle line:90%
looked like.

00:45:59.820 --> 00:46:03.310 align:middle line:84%
So similarly here,
we want to construct

00:46:03.310 --> 00:46:05.680 align:middle line:84%
a full counterfactual
distribution of prices

00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:11.500 align:middle line:84%
for markets that were treated
for the counterfactual where

00:46:11.500 --> 00:46:14.320 align:middle line:90%
the merger didn't happen.

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:17.800 align:middle line:84%
So what they're going
to use is something

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:20.000 align:middle line:84%
that Athey and Imbens
proposed in 2006.

00:46:20.000 --> 00:46:21.890 align:middle line:84%
It's called a
changes-in-changes estimator.

00:46:21.890 --> 00:46:25.000 align:middle line:84%
It's basically a generalization
of difference in differences

00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:27.110 align:middle line:84%
where you basically look
at treatment effects.

00:46:27.110 --> 00:46:30.040 align:middle line:84%
We can look at treatment
effects at different points

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:31.940 align:middle line:90%
of the distribution.

00:46:31.940 --> 00:46:38.050 align:middle line:90%


00:46:38.050 --> 00:46:40.900 align:middle line:84%
So the way this works, always
have to write it like this

00:46:40.900 --> 00:46:42.220 align:middle line:90%
to remind myself.

00:46:42.220 --> 00:46:48.910 align:middle line:84%
So the way this works is
we're asking take some margin

00:46:48.910 --> 00:46:52.870 align:middle line:84%
and look at basically
remember here

00:46:52.870 --> 00:46:57.220 align:middle line:84%
t stands for before and after.
g stands for whether or not

00:46:57.220 --> 00:46:59.660 align:middle line:90%
this is a treated market.

00:46:59.660 --> 00:47:05.980 align:middle line:84%
So we want to look for those
markets that were not treated.

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:09.400 align:middle line:90%


00:47:09.400 --> 00:47:13.930 align:middle line:84%
How large was the change in
the margin at a specific point

00:47:13.930 --> 00:47:17.064 align:middle line:90%
of the distribution?

00:47:17.064 --> 00:47:20.080 align:middle line:84%
And we basically want
that before for margin

00:47:20.080 --> 00:47:26.060 align:middle line:84%
that gives rise to this
across both markets

00:47:26.060 --> 00:47:29.000 align:middle line:84%
is the same,
because that defines

00:47:29.000 --> 00:47:31.070 align:middle line:90%
the counterfactual distribution.

00:47:31.070 --> 00:47:33.980 align:middle line:84%
So across all points
of the distribution,

00:47:33.980 --> 00:47:39.500 align:middle line:84%
we want to map from prices
after the merger has

00:47:39.500 --> 00:47:43.370 align:middle line:84%
happened to a set of
prices before the merger.

00:47:43.370 --> 00:47:45.607 align:middle line:84%
And under the
counterfactual distribution,

00:47:45.607 --> 00:47:46.690 align:middle line:90%
those have to be the same.

00:47:46.690 --> 00:47:51.080 align:middle line:90%


00:47:51.080 --> 00:47:56.690 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Why is the first
subscript a 0, 1 and not a 1, 0?

00:47:56.690 --> 00:47:57.690 align:middle line:90%
TOBIAS SALZ: Let me see.

00:47:57.690 --> 00:47:58.713 align:middle line:90%
So this here is--

00:47:58.713 --> 00:48:03.040 align:middle line:90%


00:48:03.040 --> 00:48:04.610 align:middle line:90%
yeah, this has to be 1, 0.

00:48:04.610 --> 00:48:05.900 align:middle line:90%
Good catch.

00:48:05.900 --> 00:48:06.870 align:middle line:90%
I apologize for that.

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:14.470 align:middle line:90%


00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:15.550 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:15.550 --> 00:48:16.760 align:middle line:90%
Sorry about that.

00:48:16.760 --> 00:48:19.740 align:middle line:84%
So again, we're looking on
both sides here at the change.

00:48:19.740 --> 00:48:24.248 align:middle line:90%


00:48:24.248 --> 00:48:26.140 align:middle line:84%
And again, the
counterfactual distribution

00:48:26.140 --> 00:48:28.720 align:middle line:84%
is defined such
that from any point

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:32.410 align:middle line:84%
that I look in the after
distribution, the margin

00:48:32.410 --> 00:48:37.560 align:middle line:84%
that maps to this point
has to be the same.

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:41.700 align:middle line:84%
So now I can use
this condition here.

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:43.410 align:middle line:84%
This is the object
that I do not know,

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:46.350 align:middle line:84%
which is the counterfactual
distribution of prices.

00:48:46.350 --> 00:48:54.455 align:middle line:84%
And I can apply, basically,
and yeah, here it's correct.

00:48:54.455 --> 00:48:57.660 align:middle line:90%


00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:00.550 align:middle line:84%
So I can apply f
1, 0 to both sides.

00:49:00.550 --> 00:49:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And I back out the
counterfactual distribution

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:06.995 align:middle line:90%
of prices.

00:49:06.995 --> 00:49:11.600 align:middle line:90%


00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:17.250 align:middle line:84%
So what is nice about this is
once I have this object here,

00:49:17.250 --> 00:49:19.050 align:middle line:84%
which is, again,
constructed from three

00:49:19.050 --> 00:49:22.380 align:middle line:84%
observed distributions,
I can look

00:49:22.380 --> 00:49:25.830 align:middle line:84%
at different points
of the distribution

00:49:25.830 --> 00:49:29.170 align:middle line:90%
how are consumers treated.

00:49:29.170 --> 00:49:31.710 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, I can
come back to this idea

00:49:31.710 --> 00:49:34.680 align:middle line:84%
that consumers that I
expect to search a lot

00:49:34.680 --> 00:49:38.700 align:middle line:84%
should be hurt more by
the merger than consumers

00:49:38.700 --> 00:49:39.275 align:middle line:90%
who do not.

00:49:39.275 --> 00:49:48.080 align:middle line:90%


00:49:48.080 --> 00:49:52.220 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this idea here?

00:49:52.220 --> 00:49:56.030 align:middle line:84%
So again, conceptually, it's
like difference in differences,

00:49:56.030 --> 00:49:58.950 align:middle line:84%
but you look at different
points of the distribution.

00:49:58.950 --> 00:50:01.460 align:middle line:90%


00:50:01.460 --> 00:50:07.100 align:middle line:84%
So here are the results
that we get out of this.

00:50:07.100 --> 00:50:10.250 align:middle line:84%
Again, you can look
at those either what

00:50:10.250 --> 00:50:14.990 align:middle line:84%
they call baseline or
adjusting for trends.

00:50:14.990 --> 00:50:17.930 align:middle line:84%
And what you find
here is that if you

00:50:17.930 --> 00:50:24.320 align:middle line:84%
look at the top end of the
distribution, the confidence

00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:26.310 align:middle line:90%
bands, they cross 0.

00:50:26.310 --> 00:50:28.700 align:middle line:84%
So these are all
not significant.

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:30.920 align:middle line:84%
But at the lower end of
the price distribution,

00:50:30.920 --> 00:50:32.660 align:middle line:84%
you get significant
effects at the order

00:50:32.660 --> 00:50:34.870 align:middle line:90%
of 7 to 10 basis points.

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:38.250 align:middle line:90%


00:50:38.250 --> 00:50:40.680 align:middle line:84%
So again, what is the
interpretation here?

00:50:40.680 --> 00:50:46.320 align:middle line:84%
There's some consumers in the
market that are searching a lot.

00:50:46.320 --> 00:50:50.820 align:middle line:84%
And once their choice set is
reduced, their prices go up.

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:53.440 align:middle line:84%
But consumers who were not
shopping around to begin with,

00:50:53.440 --> 00:50:55.775 align:middle line:84%
they're unaffected
by this price change.

00:50:55.775 --> 00:51:00.510 align:middle line:90%


00:51:00.510 --> 00:51:06.600 align:middle line:84%
So this leads to dispersion,
to reduction and dispersion

00:51:06.600 --> 00:51:07.560 align:middle line:90%
and prices.

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:10.350 align:middle line:84%
So different measures,
different dispersion measures

00:51:10.350 --> 00:51:13.020 align:middle line:84%
such as standard deviation,
coefficient of variation,

00:51:13.020 --> 00:51:15.743 align:middle line:84%
and interquartile range
all tell a consistent story

00:51:15.743 --> 00:51:17.160 align:middle line:84%
that prices are
getting compressed

00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:19.740 align:middle line:90%
once the merger happens.

00:51:19.740 --> 00:51:22.230 align:middle line:84%
What they also do
is they are looking

00:51:22.230 --> 00:51:24.840 align:middle line:84%
at whether or not this
counterfactual distribution

00:51:24.840 --> 00:51:29.910 align:middle line:84%
of prices that they
back out is equal

00:51:29.910 --> 00:51:32.810 align:middle line:84%
or not to the actual price
distribution that they observe.

00:51:32.810 --> 00:51:36.230 align:middle line:90%


00:51:36.230 --> 00:51:40.180 align:middle line:84%
And they can reject this with
a Kolmogorov-Smirnov test.

00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:44.290 align:middle line:84%
So basically, looking over the
full distribution of prices,

00:51:44.290 --> 00:51:47.950 align:middle line:84%
reject the null hypothesis that
the price distribution didn't

00:51:47.950 --> 00:51:50.440 align:middle line:84%
change relative to the
counterfactual price

00:51:50.440 --> 00:51:53.490 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:51:53.490 --> 00:51:57.300 align:middle line:84%
So I think this is
nice because, again, it

00:51:57.300 --> 00:52:02.190 align:middle line:84%
tells a story that's quite
consistent with the idea

00:52:02.190 --> 00:52:06.310 align:middle line:84%
of different consumers faring
very different in these markets.

00:52:06.310 --> 00:52:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And you can observe it in those
heterogeneous treatment effects.

00:52:10.340 --> 00:52:13.600 align:middle line:90%


00:52:13.600 --> 00:52:17.230 align:middle line:84%
So they now construct
a search model

00:52:17.230 --> 00:52:21.940 align:middle line:90%
to make sense of those results.

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:24.880 align:middle line:84%
And the model is constructed
so that in the end,

00:52:24.880 --> 00:52:29.800 align:middle line:84%
it maps back to these negotiated
margins in the after period.

00:52:29.800 --> 00:52:35.410 align:middle line:84%
So we have n plus 1
lenders in this market.

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:38.590 align:middle line:84%
And there are three
stages to the model.

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So initially, as
in Hong and Shum,

00:52:42.100 --> 00:52:45.120 align:middle line:84%
consumers get a
free price quote.

00:52:45.120 --> 00:52:48.310 align:middle line:84%
So you can think of this
maybe from their home lender.

00:52:48.310 --> 00:52:50.740 align:middle line:90%
I get a free quote.

00:52:50.740 --> 00:52:55.210 align:middle line:84%
I can either accept this quote
or I can reject the quote

00:52:55.210 --> 00:53:01.450 align:middle line:84%
and engage in costly effort
to gain additional quotes.

00:53:01.450 --> 00:53:08.710 align:middle line:84%
And so they're using
a search technology

00:53:08.710 --> 00:53:11.270 align:middle line:84%
that's somewhat different from
what you have seen before.

00:53:11.270 --> 00:53:14.700 align:middle line:84%
So basically, there's a
continuous effort function.

00:53:14.700 --> 00:53:18.610 align:middle line:84%
And as I increase my
continuous effort,

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:21.820 align:middle line:84%
I get a higher probability
that I'm getting more quotes

00:53:21.820 --> 00:53:23.710 align:middle line:90%
from the price distribution.

00:53:23.710 --> 00:53:29.230 align:middle line:84%
And the way that they construct
this is that with probability s,

00:53:29.230 --> 00:53:32.530 align:middle line:84%
I either get quotes from
all the firms in the market

00:53:32.530 --> 00:53:36.102 align:middle line:84%
and with probability 1 minus
s, I only get two quotes.

00:53:36.102 --> 00:53:37.810 align:middle line:84%
So this turns out to
be quite convenient,

00:53:37.810 --> 00:53:42.610 align:middle line:84%
because they can basically map
this just in continuous effort

00:53:42.610 --> 00:53:48.190 align:middle line:84%
problem instead of dealing with
something like a reservation

00:53:48.190 --> 00:53:50.650 align:middle line:84%
price strategy in the
sequential search model

00:53:50.650 --> 00:53:54.790 align:middle line:84%
or having to compare
different bins

00:53:54.790 --> 00:53:56.890 align:middle line:84%
and where consumers
sort themselves into.

00:53:56.890 --> 00:54:02.780 align:middle line:84%
So this is somewhat similar to
what people do in labor search.

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:05.510 align:middle line:84%
So I'll come back to
that comparison later on.

00:54:05.510 --> 00:54:09.820 align:middle line:84%
And it just makes the model
slightly easier to handle.

00:54:09.820 --> 00:54:13.280 align:middle line:90%


00:54:13.280 --> 00:54:16.850 align:middle line:90%
So these are the three stages.

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:18.810 align:middle line:90%
Consumers get an initial quote.

00:54:18.810 --> 00:54:21.120 align:middle line:90%
They either search or not.

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:24.320 align:middle line:84%
And when they search,
consumer, the lenders

00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:28.530 align:middle line:90%
compete for their business.

00:54:28.530 --> 00:54:29.820 align:middle line:90%
So let's work backwards.

00:54:29.820 --> 00:54:31.630 align:middle line:90%
Let's first go to stage three.

00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:34.730 align:middle line:84%
So this is where consumers went
out and get additional quotes

00:54:34.730 --> 00:54:36.920 align:middle line:90%
from the market.

00:54:36.920 --> 00:54:38.820 align:middle line:84%
They modeled this as
an English auction.

00:54:38.820 --> 00:54:40.610 align:middle line:84%
Again, this is a fairly
strong assumption,

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:47.060 align:middle line:84%
but it makes dealing with
the model somewhat easier.

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:50.420 align:middle line:84%
So lenders here have a
common cost component

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:55.960 align:middle line:84%
c and an idiosyncratic
component epsilon j.

00:54:55.960 --> 00:55:00.550 align:middle line:84%
And as you know, there is
a weakly dominant strategy

00:55:00.550 --> 00:55:04.450 align:middle line:84%
in English auctions,
which is to bid your cost.

00:55:04.450 --> 00:55:07.480 align:middle line:84%
So the lowest cost
lender wins and is

00:55:07.480 --> 00:55:09.585 align:middle line:84%
paid the price of the
second lowest cost lender.

00:55:09.585 --> 00:55:15.220 align:middle line:90%


00:55:15.220 --> 00:55:18.970 align:middle line:84%
So with this, they
can then define

00:55:18.970 --> 00:55:23.950 align:middle line:84%
an expected price, or an
expected negotiated margin,

00:55:23.950 --> 00:55:27.580 align:middle line:84%
which is, again, a function
of this common part here,

00:55:27.580 --> 00:55:30.790 align:middle line:84%
as well as the expected second
order statistic out of taking

00:55:30.790 --> 00:55:33.800 align:middle line:90%
n draws from the distribution.

00:55:33.800 --> 00:55:38.090 align:middle line:90%


00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:40.920 align:middle line:84%
And since they make this
assumption that consumers either

00:55:40.920 --> 00:55:45.300 align:middle line:84%
take two draws or n draws,
depending on whether or not

00:55:45.300 --> 00:55:48.450 align:middle line:84%
their search is successful,
you get this difference

00:55:48.450 --> 00:55:53.815 align:middle line:84%
in expected prices or delta n as
a function of how many lenders

00:55:53.815 --> 00:55:54.815 align:middle line:90%
there are in the market.

00:55:54.815 --> 00:55:57.620 align:middle line:90%


00:55:57.620 --> 00:56:01.790 align:middle line:84%
So thinking about
this, you can already

00:56:01.790 --> 00:56:04.220 align:middle line:84%
see how this maps
back to the empirics.

00:56:04.220 --> 00:56:07.640 align:middle line:84%
So in some markets,
the total number

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:13.490 align:middle line:84%
of firms that you can
search from is reduced by 1.

00:56:13.490 --> 00:56:18.560 align:middle line:84%
And of course, under this
specific search technology,

00:56:18.560 --> 00:56:21.440 align:middle line:84%
some consumers who
would have taken usually

00:56:21.440 --> 00:56:23.420 align:middle line:84%
n plus 1 draws are
now only taking n

00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:25.230 align:middle line:84%
draws when their
search is successful.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:33.090 align:middle line:90%


00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:36.840 align:middle line:84%
So this was the last stage
where the lenders are

00:56:36.840 --> 00:56:38.470 align:middle line:90%
competing against each other.

00:56:38.470 --> 00:56:43.080 align:middle line:84%
We now move to stage
two where consumers are

00:56:43.080 --> 00:56:47.040 align:middle line:90%
deciding how much to search.

00:56:47.040 --> 00:56:52.050 align:middle line:84%
And so their total search cost
here, which they call kappa i,

00:56:52.050 --> 00:56:55.410 align:middle line:84%
is a function of how
much effort they exert.

00:56:55.410 --> 00:56:58.320 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, it depends
on an idiosyncratic cost

00:56:58.320 --> 00:57:00.120 align:middle line:90%
for each consumer UI.

00:57:00.120 --> 00:57:01.910 align:middle line:84%
So this is the
consumer's marginal cost

00:57:01.910 --> 00:57:04.950 align:middle line:84%
of searching,
exerting more effort,

00:57:04.950 --> 00:57:08.640 align:middle line:90%
plus a fixed component eta i.

00:57:08.640 --> 00:57:11.510 align:middle line:84%
So if you reject your initial
quote and you go out to search,

00:57:11.510 --> 00:57:13.370 align:middle line:90%
you pay this fixed cost.

00:57:13.370 --> 00:57:16.060 align:middle line:84%
It will turn out, I'll come back
to this, that they basically

00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:17.810 align:middle line:84%
dropped this in the
empirical application,

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:19.227 align:middle line:84%
but I'll leave it
in here for now.

00:57:19.227 --> 00:57:23.320 align:middle line:90%


00:57:23.320 --> 00:57:27.430 align:middle line:84%
So an important assumption
and restriction in this model

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:31.660 align:middle line:84%
is that they assume that the
marginal cost of searching

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:34.900 align:middle line:84%
of the consumer is
publicly observed.

00:57:34.900 --> 00:57:40.810 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, the lender,
once you talk to the lender,

00:57:40.810 --> 00:57:42.610 align:middle line:84%
you walk into the
branch, they can actually

00:57:42.610 --> 00:57:45.430 align:middle line:84%
somehow learn enough
about you to know exactly

00:57:45.430 --> 00:57:47.500 align:middle line:90%
what your cost of searching is.

00:57:47.500 --> 00:57:49.640 align:middle line:84%
And this has quite
strong implications.

00:57:49.640 --> 00:57:50.680 align:middle line:90%
I'll come back to this.

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:53.260 align:middle line:84%
But this is something
important to keep in mind here.

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:57.075 align:middle line:84%
So the marginal cost of
searching is publicly observed.

00:57:57.075 --> 00:58:00.330 align:middle line:90%


00:58:00.330 --> 00:58:02.708 align:middle line:84%
They assume that there's
matching probability.

00:58:02.708 --> 00:58:04.500 align:middle line:84%
So the chance that the
search is successful

00:58:04.500 --> 00:58:06.480 align:middle line:84%
and I get n draws from
the price distribution

00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:09.700 align:middle line:84%
has this Pareto
distribution here.

00:58:09.700 --> 00:58:16.020 align:middle line:84%
And so with that, consumers
compute their reservation value,

00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:21.660 align:middle line:84%
which is the minimizer
of the search cost

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:25.560 align:middle line:84%
plus the expected
price that I get.

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:28.590 align:middle line:84%
So again, this here is
a common cost component.

00:58:28.590 --> 00:58:31.500 align:middle line:84%
If I'm not successful, I'm
getting the second order

00:58:31.500 --> 00:58:34.500 align:middle line:84%
statistics out of
taking two draws.

00:58:34.500 --> 00:58:37.050 align:middle line:84%
And when I'm successful,
I'm replacing this

00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:40.890 align:middle line:84%
with the second-order
statistic out of n draws.

00:58:40.890 --> 00:58:46.450 align:middle line:84%
This is this delta n that
we defined beforehand.

00:58:46.450 --> 00:58:49.110 align:middle line:90%
The difference in expectations.

00:58:49.110 --> 00:58:54.150 align:middle line:84%
So it will be useful to keep
track of this reservation value

00:58:54.150 --> 00:58:57.730 align:middle line:84%
as a part that is
common to all consumers

00:58:57.730 --> 00:58:59.460 align:middle line:84%
and this idiosyncratic
component here,

00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:02.100 align:middle line:84%
which depends on the
consumer's idiosyncratic search

00:59:02.100 --> 00:59:05.120 align:middle line:84%
cost, as well as the number
of lenders in the market.

00:59:05.120 --> 00:59:07.630 align:middle line:90%


00:59:07.630 --> 00:59:12.840 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, consumers are
different in terms of how costly

00:59:12.840 --> 00:59:14.290 align:middle line:90%
it is to search for them.

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:16.650 align:middle line:84%
They exert in this
continuous effort.

00:59:16.650 --> 00:59:19.500 align:middle line:84%
This gives them some
reservation value

00:59:19.500 --> 00:59:21.125 align:middle line:84%
comes out of this
optimization problem.

00:59:21.125 --> 00:59:24.830 align:middle line:90%


00:59:24.830 --> 00:59:28.260 align:middle line:84%
And there are some
consumers who will not

00:59:28.260 --> 00:59:32.345 align:middle line:84%
search at all if their
search cost is too high.

00:59:32.345 --> 00:59:36.100 align:middle line:90%


00:59:36.100 --> 00:59:39.550 align:middle line:84%
So we are now
moving to stage one,

00:59:39.550 --> 00:59:45.567 align:middle line:84%
which is the lender who gives
the initial quote decides

00:59:45.567 --> 00:59:46.650 align:middle line:90%
what that quote should be.

00:59:46.650 --> 00:59:52.395 align:middle line:90%


00:59:52.395 --> 00:59:56.080 align:middle line:84%
So for that, we define
the distribution

00:59:56.080 --> 00:59:59.440 align:middle line:84%
of eta, which was this
fixed cost of searching.

00:59:59.440 --> 01:00:04.600 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer who decides
whether or not to take more

01:00:04.600 --> 01:00:08.355 align:middle line:84%
draws privately observes
this fixed cost.

01:00:08.355 --> 01:00:11.260 align:middle line:90%


01:00:11.260 --> 01:00:14.710 align:middle line:84%
And the probability that
the consumer goes out

01:00:14.710 --> 01:00:17.260 align:middle line:84%
to take more draws
is the probability

01:00:17.260 --> 01:00:21.730 align:middle line:84%
that eta i plus the
reservation value

01:00:21.730 --> 01:00:26.050 align:middle line:84%
is lower than the
initial quote that I get.

01:00:26.050 --> 01:00:28.210 align:middle line:84%
The lender observes
ui, so the lender

01:00:28.210 --> 01:00:30.790 align:middle line:84%
knows what your
reservation value is.

01:00:30.790 --> 01:00:33.110 align:middle line:84%
But the lender does
not know eta i.

01:00:33.110 --> 01:00:35.385 align:middle line:90%
So this is privately observed.

01:00:35.385 --> 01:00:40.360 align:middle line:90%


01:00:40.360 --> 01:00:43.720 align:middle line:84%
So the lender who
sets the initial quote

01:00:43.720 --> 01:00:47.200 align:middle line:84%
maximizes, again, the
margin times the probability

01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:49.415 align:middle line:84%
that the consumer
does not walk away.

01:00:49.415 --> 01:00:51.040 align:middle line:84%
And there's an implicit
assumption here

01:00:51.040 --> 01:00:53.290 align:middle line:84%
that when the consumer
walks away, then

01:00:53.290 --> 01:00:55.900 align:middle line:84%
I'm losing the business
of the consumer for sure.

01:00:55.900 --> 01:00:59.680 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer then
takes one of the offers

01:00:59.680 --> 01:01:01.690 align:middle line:84%
from the other lenders
and doesn't compare this

01:01:01.690 --> 01:01:03.780 align:middle line:90%
to my initial m0 quote.

01:01:03.780 --> 01:01:09.290 align:middle line:90%


01:01:09.290 --> 01:01:15.020 align:middle line:84%
So this is basically a
monopoly pricing problem,

01:01:15.020 --> 01:01:19.160 align:middle line:84%
where I'm pricing against this
residual demand curve here,

01:01:19.160 --> 01:01:23.360 align:middle line:84%
which is whether or not the
consumer walks away or not.

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:25.700 align:middle line:84%
And you get this
solution here where

01:01:25.700 --> 01:01:29.620 align:middle line:84%
the margin is equal to
the inverse hazard rate.

01:01:29.620 --> 01:01:33.200 align:middle line:84%
So a pretty standard
solution for these problems

01:01:33.200 --> 01:01:35.480 align:middle line:84%
where consumers have
private information.

01:01:35.480 --> 01:01:37.910 align:middle line:84%
So the consumer earns
some information rent,

01:01:37.910 --> 01:01:42.110 align:middle line:84%
and that leads in principle
to somewhat lower prices

01:01:42.110 --> 01:01:44.490 align:middle line:84%
than the reservation
value would imply.

01:01:44.490 --> 01:01:48.710 align:middle line:84%
In the empirical
application, however, they're

01:01:48.710 --> 01:01:51.943 align:middle line:90%
going to just set eta i to 0.

01:01:51.943 --> 01:01:53.360 align:middle line:84%
So the empirical
application, they

01:01:53.360 --> 01:01:56.360 align:middle line:84%
assume that consumers do
not earn any information

01:01:56.360 --> 01:01:58.460 align:middle line:84%
rent and lenders are
therefore just paying them

01:01:58.460 --> 01:02:00.950 align:middle line:84%
their reservation value,
which is because they

01:02:00.950 --> 01:02:02.485 align:middle line:90%
observe ui publicly observed.

01:02:02.485 --> 01:02:11.890 align:middle line:90%


01:02:11.890 --> 01:02:17.770 align:middle line:84%
OK, so with this solution,
where there is basically now,

01:02:17.770 --> 01:02:21.070 align:middle line:84%
according to this very
stark restriction, no more

01:02:21.070 --> 01:02:23.710 align:middle line:84%
any private information
of consumers,

01:02:23.710 --> 01:02:27.010 align:middle line:84%
you can directly
map what comes out

01:02:27.010 --> 01:02:32.020 align:middle line:84%
of this search problem of
the consumer to the observed

01:02:32.020 --> 01:02:35.220 align:middle line:84%
distribution of negotiated
margins in the after period.

01:02:35.220 --> 01:02:39.500 align:middle line:84%
So remember, this here is
just for the treated markets

01:02:39.500 --> 01:02:40.840 align:middle line:90%
in the after period.

01:02:40.840 --> 01:02:45.220 align:middle line:84%
The fraction of consumers who
get a negotiated margin less

01:02:45.220 --> 01:02:46.930 align:middle line:90%
than m.

01:02:46.930 --> 01:02:49.270 align:middle line:84%
And we can write this
as the probability

01:02:49.270 --> 01:02:52.240 align:middle line:84%
that this reservation
value, which

01:02:52.240 --> 01:02:54.880 align:middle line:84%
had the common component and
the consumer idiosyncratic

01:02:54.880 --> 01:02:57.610 align:middle line:90%
component, is less than m.

01:02:57.610 --> 01:03:01.720 align:middle line:84%
And if we define hu
to be the distribution

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:04.060 align:middle line:84%
of these idiosyncratic
search costs,

01:03:04.060 --> 01:03:06.730 align:middle line:84%
we can instead rewrite
this as follows.

01:03:06.730 --> 01:03:14.218 align:middle line:84%
So we can write the quantiles
of the consumer types, which is

01:03:14.218 --> 01:03:15.510 align:middle line:90%
how costly it is to search for.

01:03:15.510 --> 01:03:17.885 align:middle line:84%
You have to correspond to the
quantiles of the negotiated

01:03:17.885 --> 01:03:19.550 align:middle line:90%
margin.

01:03:19.550 --> 01:03:22.610 align:middle line:84%
So this is a strategy
that you will also

01:03:22.610 --> 01:03:25.880 align:middle line:84%
see in auctions where there's
something kind of idiosyncratic

01:03:25.880 --> 01:03:27.180 align:middle line:90%
that's unobserved.

01:03:27.180 --> 01:03:30.110 align:middle line:84%
And consumers are
ordered in some way.

01:03:30.110 --> 01:03:32.450 align:middle line:84%
And that's going
to map to something

01:03:32.450 --> 01:03:33.890 align:middle line:90%
observed that's kind of ordered.

01:03:33.890 --> 01:03:37.050 align:middle line:84%
In this case, the price
that consumers get.

01:03:37.050 --> 01:03:42.930 align:middle line:84%
And again, the way this works
here is I have my search costs.

01:03:42.930 --> 01:03:44.900 align:middle line:84%
The lender observes
that and just pays me

01:03:44.900 --> 01:03:46.410 align:middle line:90%
off my reservation value.

01:03:46.410 --> 01:03:49.190 align:middle line:84%
And so you get this
kind of perfect ordering

01:03:49.190 --> 01:03:51.080 align:middle line:84%
of the negotiated
margin that corresponds

01:03:51.080 --> 01:03:52.945 align:middle line:84%
to the ordering of
these unobserved types.

01:03:52.945 --> 01:03:59.730 align:middle line:90%


01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:04.770 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this here only works
for a given set of parameters

01:04:04.770 --> 01:04:09.870 align:middle line:84%
of the remaining problem here,
which still includes the firm's

01:04:09.870 --> 01:04:13.770 align:middle line:84%
cost variation as
well as lambda i,

01:04:13.770 --> 01:04:16.980 align:middle line:84%
which was determining how costly
it is for consumers to search.

01:04:16.980 --> 01:04:23.040 align:middle line:84%
So we can't quite directly
back out this distribution hu,

01:04:23.040 --> 01:04:25.740 align:middle line:84%
because we don't know yet
the parameters, the remaining

01:04:25.740 --> 01:04:30.090 align:middle line:84%
parameters of this
problem, which

01:04:30.090 --> 01:04:35.045 align:middle line:84%
are the search effort parameters
as well as the cost of lenders.

01:04:35.045 --> 01:04:39.410 align:middle line:90%


01:04:39.410 --> 01:04:44.480 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that
you get out of this

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:50.060 align:middle line:84%
is that consumers that
have higher search costs

01:04:50.060 --> 01:04:53.190 align:middle line:84%
benefit less from the merger
than consumers with lower search

01:04:53.190 --> 01:04:53.690 align:middle line:90%
costs.

01:04:53.690 --> 01:04:58.460 align:middle line:84%
So for u prime larger
u, you get a difference

01:04:58.460 --> 01:05:03.650 align:middle line:84%
in reservation values for
less sophisticated consumers

01:05:03.650 --> 01:05:06.740 align:middle line:84%
that's smaller
than for consumers

01:05:06.740 --> 01:05:09.140 align:middle line:90%
that have a lower search cost.

01:05:09.140 --> 01:05:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So in other words, what
this model here generates

01:05:11.810 --> 01:05:15.168 align:middle line:84%
is this difference that
we already saw in the data

01:05:15.168 --> 01:05:17.210 align:middle line:84%
that we see larger treatment
effects at the lower

01:05:17.210 --> 01:05:19.160 align:middle line:84%
end of the price
distribution, where

01:05:19.160 --> 01:05:21.980 align:middle line:84%
we would expect to be the
consumers that have lower search

01:05:21.980 --> 01:05:25.500 align:middle line:84%
costs relative to at the high
of the price distribution.

01:05:25.500 --> 01:05:28.564 align:middle line:90%


01:05:28.564 --> 01:05:34.260 align:middle line:84%
So in other words,
this pricing function

01:05:34.260 --> 01:05:38.640 align:middle line:84%
under symmetric information
basically is monotonically

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:41.820 align:middle line:90%
increasing in ui.

01:05:41.820 --> 01:05:43.260 align:middle line:90%
It's weakly decreasing in n.

01:05:43.260 --> 01:05:45.690 align:middle line:90%
So competition helps.

01:05:45.690 --> 01:05:50.400 align:middle line:84%
And there are these increasing
marginal effects of the merger

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:53.345 align:middle line:84%
when you get to consumers
of lower search costs.

01:05:53.345 --> 01:05:58.540 align:middle line:90%


01:05:58.540 --> 01:06:05.140 align:middle line:84%
So with all of this put
together, what they're doing is

01:06:05.140 --> 01:06:10.360 align:middle line:84%
they now want to explain the
actual observed differences

01:06:10.360 --> 01:06:12.520 align:middle line:90%
in the treatment effects.

01:06:12.520 --> 01:06:16.600 align:middle line:84%
So we had basically
large treatment effects

01:06:16.600 --> 01:06:19.420 align:middle line:84%
at the bottom of the price
distribution, small treatment

01:06:19.420 --> 01:06:20.660 align:middle line:90%
effects at the top.

01:06:20.660 --> 01:06:25.210 align:middle line:84%
And they're going
to find parameters,

01:06:25.210 --> 01:06:29.260 align:middle line:84%
which is the common cost of
lenders, the consumer search

01:06:29.260 --> 01:06:32.350 align:middle line:84%
strategy, as well as the
variation of the idiosyncratic

01:06:32.350 --> 01:06:36.700 align:middle line:84%
cost of lenders,
that best explain

01:06:36.700 --> 01:06:40.900 align:middle line:84%
the differences in negotiated
margins going from before to

01:06:40.900 --> 01:06:42.690 align:middle line:90%
after the merger.

01:06:42.690 --> 01:06:48.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is, I think, quite
nice because they basically

01:06:48.190 --> 01:06:51.460 align:middle line:84%
let the model be informed
by this treatment effect

01:06:51.460 --> 01:06:54.070 align:middle line:90%
that they observe in the data.

01:06:54.070 --> 01:07:01.240 align:middle line:84%
And so because of admittedly
a fairly stark assumption,

01:07:01.240 --> 01:07:05.860 align:middle line:84%
they get this direct mapping
between the observed negotiated

01:07:05.860 --> 01:07:09.580 align:middle line:84%
margin and what is unobserved
to the researcher, which

01:07:09.580 --> 01:07:11.870 align:middle line:84%
is the type distribution
of consumers.

01:07:11.870 --> 01:07:19.850 align:middle line:84%
They can impose basically that
quantiles of consumer types

01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:22.318 align:middle line:84%
have to correspond
to this object

01:07:22.318 --> 01:07:23.610 align:middle line:90%
that they get out of the model.

01:07:23.610 --> 01:07:25.550 align:middle line:84%
So they're basically
directly imposing

01:07:25.550 --> 01:07:27.230 align:middle line:84%
the fact that these
observed margins

01:07:27.230 --> 01:07:31.510 align:middle line:84%
have to be equal to these
unobserved consumer types.

01:07:31.510 --> 01:07:37.640 align:middle line:90%


01:07:37.640 --> 01:07:41.630 align:middle line:84%
So again, the way this
works is they only

01:07:41.630 --> 01:07:44.300 align:middle line:84%
have these three
parameters here.

01:07:44.300 --> 01:07:46.320 align:middle line:84%
This is a method
of moments problem.

01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:49.430 align:middle line:84%
And for each time they evaluate
the objective function,

01:07:49.430 --> 01:07:52.310 align:middle line:84%
they directly impose
this correspondence

01:07:52.310 --> 01:07:56.170 align:middle line:84%
between observed margins and
unobserved consumer types.

01:07:56.170 --> 01:08:01.240 align:middle line:90%


01:08:01.240 --> 01:08:03.680 align:middle line:84%
So what do they get
out of this in the end?

01:08:03.680 --> 01:08:07.150 align:middle line:84%
So one thing that's
typically, of course,

01:08:07.150 --> 01:08:09.100 align:middle line:84%
when people compute
structural models,

01:08:09.100 --> 01:08:11.780 align:middle line:84%
they use them to
compute counterfactuals.

01:08:11.780 --> 01:08:14.630 align:middle line:84%
Here, the counterfactual is
already observed in the data.

01:08:14.630 --> 01:08:17.770 align:middle line:84%
So the additional thing that you
get out of computing this model

01:08:17.770 --> 01:08:20.859 align:middle line:84%
is that you can learn something
about the unobserved search

01:08:20.859 --> 01:08:22.130 align:middle line:90%
costs of consumers.

01:08:22.130 --> 01:08:27.850 align:middle line:84%
They estimate that this is about
$1,700 for an average loan size

01:08:27.850 --> 01:08:32.140 align:middle line:90%
of about $152,000.

01:08:32.140 --> 01:08:35.140 align:middle line:84%
And because they also get
lenders' costs from this,

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:37.479 align:middle line:84%
they can compute
their markups, which

01:08:37.479 --> 01:08:41.859 align:middle line:84%
are at the order of
5% to 10% And so,

01:08:41.859 --> 01:08:45.550 align:middle line:84%
again, this model, I mean,
it's constructed in this way,

01:08:45.550 --> 01:08:48.460 align:middle line:84%
but it directly maps to
this empirical observation.

01:08:48.460 --> 01:08:52.390 align:middle line:84%
The consumers at the bottom
end of the price distribution

01:08:52.390 --> 01:08:54.819 align:middle line:84%
are hurt more by the
merger than consumers

01:08:54.819 --> 01:08:56.969 align:middle line:90%
who are searching much less.

01:08:56.969 --> 01:09:03.600 align:middle line:90%


01:09:03.600 --> 01:09:06.720 align:middle line:84%
I think this is a very nice
paper, because again, it

01:09:06.720 --> 01:09:12.540 align:middle line:84%
uses an actual change
in market structure

01:09:12.540 --> 01:09:13.870 align:middle line:90%
that's observed in the data.

01:09:13.870 --> 01:09:16.050 align:middle line:90%
So an actual merger.

01:09:16.050 --> 01:09:19.590 align:middle line:84%
It highlights what I think
is a new angle on the effects

01:09:19.590 --> 01:09:22.870 align:middle line:84%
of mergers, is that different
consumers fare very differently.

01:09:22.870 --> 01:09:26.010 align:middle line:84%
And that could potentially mean
that we underestimate the market

01:09:26.010 --> 01:09:27.310 align:middle line:90%
power effect of a merger.

01:09:27.310 --> 01:09:31.710 align:middle line:84%
Because if, let's say,
consumers don't search very much

01:09:31.710 --> 01:09:35.100 align:middle line:84%
or we have markets in which
search costs are very high,

01:09:35.100 --> 01:09:38.220 align:middle line:84%
and we do, let's say, a simple
before-after merger analysis,

01:09:38.220 --> 01:09:41.010 align:middle line:84%
we might observe that prices
don't change all that much.

01:09:41.010 --> 01:09:44.010 align:middle line:84%
But that could be because
consumers are just not searching

01:09:44.010 --> 01:09:44.645 align:middle line:90%
very much.

01:09:44.645 --> 01:09:46.020 align:middle line:84%
And so to the
extent that we want

01:09:46.020 --> 01:09:48.460 align:middle line:84%
to learn what's the market
power effect of a merger,

01:09:48.460 --> 01:09:51.720 align:middle line:84%
it's important to
account for that.

01:09:51.720 --> 01:09:54.149 align:middle line:84%
And it provides
this what I think

01:09:54.149 --> 01:09:57.168 align:middle line:84%
is very tight link between
the treatment effect analysis

01:09:57.168 --> 01:09:58.210 align:middle line:90%
and the structural model.

01:09:58.210 --> 01:10:00.700 align:middle line:84%
And that, of course,
required some,

01:10:00.700 --> 01:10:06.334 align:middle line:84%
as I think I've pointed out,
fairly strong assumptions.

01:10:06.334 --> 01:10:10.360 align:middle line:84%
In particular, to come
back to those, the fact

01:10:10.360 --> 01:10:12.760 align:middle line:84%
that there are no
information asymmetries

01:10:12.760 --> 01:10:16.090 align:middle line:84%
between the borrowers
and the lenders

01:10:16.090 --> 01:10:21.100 align:middle line:84%
means that search is actually
not happening in equilibrium.

01:10:21.100 --> 01:10:26.020 align:middle line:84%
So the lenders-- think about
the quote of the initial lender.

01:10:26.020 --> 01:10:28.000 align:middle line:84%
The lender is always
paying consumers such

01:10:28.000 --> 01:10:31.570 align:middle line:84%
that they do not find it worth
their while to go out and get

01:10:31.570 --> 01:10:33.640 align:middle line:90%
additional quotes.

01:10:33.640 --> 01:10:39.160 align:middle line:84%
So that means if you want to,
for instance, quantify the,

01:10:39.160 --> 01:10:42.340 align:middle line:84%
let's say, lost economic
surplus due to search costs,

01:10:42.340 --> 01:10:44.710 align:middle line:84%
this wouldn't be the
right model for that.

01:10:44.710 --> 01:10:51.070 align:middle line:84%
And we might think that
in some markets, the fact

01:10:51.070 --> 01:10:53.770 align:middle line:84%
that some consumers
have lower search

01:10:53.770 --> 01:10:57.040 align:middle line:84%
costs than others can have
disciplined prices in the market

01:10:57.040 --> 01:10:58.638 align:middle line:90%
overall.

01:10:58.638 --> 01:11:01.180 align:middle line:84%
So you had seen this effect,
for instance, in the style model

01:11:01.180 --> 01:11:03.850 align:middle line:84%
that if you get more
sophisticated consumers

01:11:03.850 --> 01:11:07.910 align:middle line:84%
in the market, we move towards
more competitive pricing.

01:11:07.910 --> 01:11:10.760 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of effect
you don't get here

01:11:10.760 --> 01:11:14.000 align:middle line:84%
because of this perfect price
discrimination where every type

01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:15.055 align:middle line:90%
gets kind of his price.

01:11:15.055 --> 01:11:17.734 align:middle line:90%


01:11:17.734 --> 01:11:21.190 align:middle line:84%
So I think that's a
potential shortcoming

01:11:21.190 --> 01:11:27.860 align:middle line:84%
of this model that is kind
of interesting to explore.

01:11:27.860 --> 01:11:30.070 align:middle line:84%
But I think they
had good reasons

01:11:30.070 --> 01:11:31.570 align:middle line:84%
to construct it
this way, because it

01:11:31.570 --> 01:11:37.640 align:middle line:84%
provided this tight link to
the empirical specification.

01:11:37.640 --> 01:11:40.750 align:middle line:90%


01:11:40.750 --> 01:11:43.450 align:middle line:84%
Any questions or comments
about this paper?

01:11:43.450 --> 01:11:44.200 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:11:44.200 --> 01:11:47.440 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I guess here
the search costs are just

01:11:47.440 --> 01:11:50.110 align:middle line:90%
based on each consumer.

01:11:50.110 --> 01:11:54.290 align:middle line:84%
There's nothing that the firm
does to change the search costs.

01:11:54.290 --> 01:11:56.680 align:middle line:84%
But it seems like in
a mortgage market,

01:11:56.680 --> 01:11:59.890 align:middle line:84%
I could imagine that maybe
the firm does less advertising

01:11:59.890 --> 01:12:02.660 align:middle line:84%
or I make you walk into my
office rather than call me.

01:12:02.660 --> 01:12:06.100 align:middle line:84%
And that would be, like,
if [INAUDIBLE] strategy

01:12:06.100 --> 01:12:08.410 align:middle line:84%
for changing the search
cost depending on how

01:12:08.410 --> 01:12:10.210 align:middle line:90%
much competition there is.

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:12.970 align:middle line:84%
And so what is the
reasoning for trying

01:12:12.970 --> 01:12:16.720 align:middle line:84%
to think of firm strategies
just in the prices

01:12:16.720 --> 01:12:19.600 align:middle line:84%
that they give consumers rather
than any way in which they could

01:12:19.600 --> 01:12:22.870 align:middle line:90%
be changing the search costs?

01:12:22.870 --> 01:12:25.420 align:middle line:84%
TOBIAS SALZ: So
first of all, there

01:12:25.420 --> 01:12:28.090 align:middle line:84%
are papers mostly in
the marketing literature

01:12:28.090 --> 01:12:31.510 align:middle line:84%
that actually do
what you suggest.

01:12:31.510 --> 01:12:34.750 align:middle line:84%
They think about advertising
as basically firms

01:12:34.750 --> 01:12:41.030 align:middle line:84%
providing a search, a
free quote, to consumers.

01:12:41.030 --> 01:12:43.240 align:middle line:84%
There's actually a fairly
recent paper by Andrea,

01:12:43.240 --> 01:12:46.120 align:middle line:84%
Siminoff, and Raluca
Ursu, and some others

01:12:46.120 --> 01:12:50.320 align:middle line:84%
that do exactly that,
where they try to see

01:12:50.320 --> 01:12:56.950 align:middle line:84%
how does a free quote or free
presentation of a product

01:12:56.950 --> 01:12:59.030 align:middle line:84%
influence a consumer's
search strategy?

01:12:59.030 --> 01:13:00.730 align:middle line:84%
And they do this
with online data.

01:13:00.730 --> 01:13:02.505 align:middle line:84%
So people have
thought about this.

01:13:02.505 --> 01:13:06.190 align:middle line:90%


01:13:06.190 --> 01:13:09.610 align:middle line:90%
And I think it's interesting.

01:13:09.610 --> 01:13:14.590 align:middle line:84%
I think in part,
it's tractability.

01:13:14.590 --> 01:13:16.050 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, that's a good thought.

01:13:16.050 --> 01:13:19.550 align:middle line:90%


01:13:19.550 --> 01:13:21.480 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions about this?

01:13:21.480 --> 01:13:28.190 align:middle line:90%


01:13:28.190 --> 01:13:31.910 align:middle line:84%
So I'll probably not
get through all of this,

01:13:31.910 --> 01:13:35.000 align:middle line:84%
but I just want to very briefly
talk about my job market

01:13:35.000 --> 01:13:41.690 align:middle line:84%
paper, which ties in quite
nicely with this previous paper

01:13:41.690 --> 01:13:44.210 align:middle line:84%
that we discussed,
because in some sense, one

01:13:44.210 --> 01:13:46.010 align:middle line:84%
of the kind of
economic differences

01:13:46.010 --> 01:13:53.090 align:middle line:84%
that I'm after here is precisely
getting at these spillover

01:13:53.090 --> 01:13:55.910 align:middle line:84%
effects of having more
sophisticated consumers

01:13:55.910 --> 01:13:56.830 align:middle line:90%
in the market.

01:13:56.830 --> 01:14:00.410 align:middle line:84%
So again, the idea,
which is, for instance,

01:14:00.410 --> 01:14:04.520 align:middle line:84%
in the Salop-Stiglitz
model also,

01:14:04.520 --> 01:14:08.330 align:middle line:84%
that when there are
sophisticated consumers

01:14:08.330 --> 01:14:10.440 align:middle line:84%
in the market, both
sophisticated and

01:14:10.440 --> 01:14:12.440 align:middle line:84%
unsophisticated ones, and
firms can actually not

01:14:12.440 --> 01:14:14.120 align:middle line:90%
tell them apart.

01:14:14.120 --> 01:14:17.690 align:middle line:84%
Then the fact that some
consumers are searching

01:14:17.690 --> 01:14:20.120 align:middle line:84%
disciplines the firm's
pricing, because they

01:14:20.120 --> 01:14:22.587 align:middle line:84%
don't know which kinds of
consumers they're dealing with.

01:14:22.587 --> 01:14:24.170 align:middle line:84%
And this effect was
kind of completely

01:14:24.170 --> 01:14:27.160 align:middle line:90%
shut down in the previous paper.

01:14:27.160 --> 01:14:30.390 align:middle line:84%
And so in particular,
I want to look

01:14:30.390 --> 01:14:36.150 align:middle line:84%
at this effect with an
application to intermediaries.

01:14:36.150 --> 01:14:38.940 align:middle line:84%
So intermediaries are
in this setting firms

01:14:38.940 --> 01:14:41.640 align:middle line:90%
that help buyers search.

01:14:41.640 --> 01:14:45.000 align:middle line:84%
And if you think about
who are the buyers that

01:14:45.000 --> 01:14:48.757 align:middle line:84%
would hire somebody else to
conduct their search for them,

01:14:48.757 --> 01:14:51.090 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be buyers that
have very, very high search

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:52.410 align:middle line:90%
costs.

01:14:52.410 --> 01:14:54.180 align:middle line:84%
So you could construct
a model where

01:14:54.180 --> 01:14:58.350 align:middle line:84%
there's some sorting very
closely related to the Hong

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:01.020 align:middle line:84%
and Shum model that I showed
you, where you have basically

01:15:01.020 --> 01:15:03.640 align:middle line:90%
these different consumer bins.

01:15:03.640 --> 01:15:05.130 align:middle line:84%
Consumers with
higher search costs

01:15:05.130 --> 01:15:07.800 align:middle line:84%
take more draws from
the price distribution.

01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:10.030 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, take fewer draws
from the price distribution.

01:15:10.030 --> 01:15:11.655 align:middle line:84%
Once your search cost
becomes too high,

01:15:11.655 --> 01:15:14.130 align:middle line:90%
you instead use an intermediary.

01:15:14.130 --> 01:15:18.450 align:middle line:84%
And so what we
get out of this is

01:15:18.450 --> 01:15:21.210 align:middle line:84%
that the highest
search cost types

01:15:21.210 --> 01:15:23.430 align:middle line:90%
are kind of leaving the market.

01:15:23.430 --> 01:15:25.710 align:middle line:84%
They're going instead
through the intermediary.

01:15:25.710 --> 01:15:33.260 align:middle line:84%
And firms in their pricing will
kind of condition on the fact

01:15:33.260 --> 01:15:36.470 align:middle line:84%
that in equilibrium, they're not
encountering the very highest

01:15:36.470 --> 01:15:39.310 align:middle line:90%
search cost types.

01:15:39.310 --> 01:15:43.540 align:middle line:84%
So again, think about the
Hong and Shum search-cost

01:15:43.540 --> 01:15:44.290 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

01:15:44.290 --> 01:15:47.840 align:middle line:84%
You have at the very top the
people that leave the market.

01:15:47.840 --> 01:15:50.410 align:middle line:84%
And that could lead to lower
prices for all consumers

01:15:50.410 --> 01:15:52.390 align:middle line:90%
in the market.

01:15:52.390 --> 01:15:54.760 align:middle line:84%
And so this, of
course, only works

01:15:54.760 --> 01:15:57.520 align:middle line:84%
if you now do have this private
information, if firms do not

01:15:57.520 --> 01:15:59.380 align:middle line:90%
know whom they're dealing with.

01:15:59.380 --> 01:16:03.190 align:middle line:84%
And that introduces some
technical challenges

01:16:03.190 --> 01:16:05.980 align:middle line:90%
in how to deal with that.

01:16:05.980 --> 01:16:09.070 align:middle line:84%
And so basically what
I do in this paper

01:16:09.070 --> 01:16:12.790 align:middle line:84%
is construct a model that
essentially puts together

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:19.390 align:middle line:84%
the Hong and Shum search
strategy of consumers, where

01:16:19.390 --> 01:16:21.190 align:middle line:84%
consumers have
heterogeneous search costs

01:16:21.190 --> 01:16:23.190 align:middle line:84%
and decide how many draws
to take from the price

01:16:23.190 --> 01:16:26.080 align:middle line:84%
distribution, with
a model of suppliers

01:16:26.080 --> 01:16:30.320 align:middle line:84%
that also have heterogeneous
costs for providing services.

01:16:30.320 --> 01:16:32.980 align:middle line:84%
And in these kinds
of settings, you now

01:16:32.980 --> 01:16:36.760 align:middle line:84%
have unobserved
source of variation

01:16:36.760 --> 01:16:39.640 align:middle line:90%
on both sides of the market.

01:16:39.640 --> 01:16:44.830 align:middle line:84%
So again, one
contribution is here

01:16:44.830 --> 01:16:49.540 align:middle line:84%
to construct a model for these
kinds of decentralized markets

01:16:49.540 --> 01:16:52.720 align:middle line:84%
where firms don't know
what type of consumer

01:16:52.720 --> 01:16:53.890 align:middle line:90%
they're dealing with.

01:16:53.890 --> 01:16:56.920 align:middle line:84%
And one data contribution
is to find a setting

01:16:56.920 --> 01:17:01.250 align:middle line:84%
where you actually find prices
for decentralized markets.

01:17:01.250 --> 01:17:04.610 align:middle line:84%
So typically good settings
are those that are regulated.

01:17:04.610 --> 01:17:08.883 align:middle line:84%
So privacy, in the case
of the mortgage paper.

01:17:08.883 --> 01:17:10.300 align:middle line:84%
The reason that
they had this data

01:17:10.300 --> 01:17:12.870 align:middle line:84%
is because people
needed to get insurance.

01:17:12.870 --> 01:17:15.550 align:middle line:84%
So they collected what is
essentially administrative data,

01:17:15.550 --> 01:17:21.860 align:middle line:84%
because the lenders had to
submit this to a regulator.

01:17:21.860 --> 01:17:24.430 align:middle line:84%
And so a similar
thing in my setting

01:17:24.430 --> 01:17:27.910 align:middle line:84%
here where I'm going to use data
from the New York City trade

01:17:27.910 --> 01:17:31.090 align:middle line:84%
waste market, which were very
idiosyncratic reasons, which

01:17:31.090 --> 01:17:34.870 align:middle line:84%
is basically organized crime
in the city of new York

01:17:34.870 --> 01:17:37.398 align:middle line:90%
was starting to be regulated.

01:17:37.398 --> 01:17:39.190 align:middle line:84%
And all the firms have
to submit their data

01:17:39.190 --> 01:17:41.610 align:middle line:90%
to a central regulator.

01:17:41.610 --> 01:17:47.360 align:middle line:84%
And so you can actually see
individual level contract data

01:17:47.360 --> 01:17:49.460 align:middle line:84%
that allow you to
make some progress

01:17:49.460 --> 01:17:51.070 align:middle line:90%
on these kinds of markets.

01:17:51.070 --> 01:17:54.510 align:middle line:90%


01:17:54.510 --> 01:17:59.060 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is the
non-residential waste disposal

01:17:59.060 --> 01:17:59.560 align:middle line:90%
market.

01:17:59.560 --> 01:18:02.340 align:middle line:84%
So trade waste market is
a fancy name for that.

01:18:02.340 --> 01:18:05.280 align:middle line:84%
And so what is really
striking about this market

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:08.700 align:middle line:84%
is that there are a lot of
sellers in New York City

01:18:08.700 --> 01:18:11.620 align:middle line:84%
relative to most other
cities in the US.

01:18:11.620 --> 01:18:15.870 align:middle line:84%
So in most other cities,
you have large companies

01:18:15.870 --> 01:18:17.670 align:middle line:84%
like waste management
and others that

01:18:17.670 --> 01:18:19.830 align:middle line:90%
are supplying the entire city.

01:18:19.830 --> 01:18:25.450 align:middle line:84%
Here you have about 100 sellers
in some zip codes of Manhattan.

01:18:25.450 --> 01:18:29.670 align:middle line:90%
You can call up to 35 carters.

01:18:29.670 --> 01:18:31.780 align:middle line:84%
So there seems to be
this very fragmented

01:18:31.780 --> 01:18:36.103 align:middle line:84%
and, from an outside observers
point of view, redundant supply.

01:18:36.103 --> 01:18:42.220 align:middle line:90%


01:18:42.220 --> 01:18:44.740 align:middle line:84%
The market is regulated
by the Business Integrity

01:18:44.740 --> 01:18:49.090 align:middle line:84%
Commission, which was
established by the city in 1995.

01:18:49.090 --> 01:18:53.530 align:middle line:84%
And again, was a response to
the property and racketeering

01:18:53.530 --> 01:18:55.750 align:middle line:84%
system that has been
in place in New York.

01:18:55.750 --> 01:19:00.250 align:middle line:84%
Famously, many crime shows and
movies, that the waste market

01:19:00.250 --> 01:19:04.240 align:middle line:84%
was one of the predominant
industries where

01:19:04.240 --> 01:19:05.750 align:middle line:90%
organized crime was active.

01:19:05.750 --> 01:19:10.510 align:middle line:84%
And that led the city to
actually collect all this data.

01:19:10.510 --> 01:19:12.370 align:middle line:84%
So I want to use
my last few minutes

01:19:12.370 --> 01:19:15.490 align:middle line:84%
to just show you
quickly the model

01:19:15.490 --> 01:19:18.640 align:middle line:84%
and how it works just
so you get a sense.

01:19:18.640 --> 01:19:21.850 align:middle line:84%
It's a bit fast, but just
to get you the idea of what

01:19:21.850 --> 01:19:23.860 align:middle line:90%
is different about this model.

01:19:23.860 --> 01:19:27.100 align:middle line:90%
So here again, we have buyers.

01:19:27.100 --> 01:19:28.750 align:middle line:84%
These are businesses
in New York City

01:19:28.750 --> 01:19:31.790 align:middle line:84%
that need to contract for
their waste disposal services.

01:19:31.790 --> 01:19:32.810 align:middle line:90%
We have sellers.

01:19:32.810 --> 01:19:35.420 align:middle line:90%
These are carting companies.

01:19:35.420 --> 01:19:37.900 align:middle line:84%
And then we have
brokers in the middle.

01:19:37.900 --> 01:19:39.610 align:middle line:84%
And some buyers could
decide to delegate

01:19:39.610 --> 01:19:42.760 align:middle line:84%
to a broker and the broker
instead searches for them,

01:19:42.760 --> 01:19:45.730 align:middle line:84%
but by searching here
means it's an auction.

01:19:45.730 --> 01:19:48.760 align:middle line:84%
And so what is challenging
about this model

01:19:48.760 --> 01:19:53.770 align:middle line:84%
is that we have basically two
sources of unobserved variation

01:19:53.770 --> 01:19:56.740 align:middle line:84%
that gives rise to prices,
which are unobserved

01:19:56.740 --> 01:20:00.760 align:middle line:84%
costs of businesses to provide
service and the unobserved

01:20:00.760 --> 01:20:05.110 align:middle line:84%
search costs of
buyers to search,

01:20:05.110 --> 01:20:08.380 align:middle line:90%
which are here called kappa.

01:20:08.380 --> 01:20:11.290 align:middle line:84%
And so if you, for
instance, contrast this

01:20:11.290 --> 01:20:14.050 align:middle line:84%
with the Hong and Shum
model, in Hong and Shum,

01:20:14.050 --> 01:20:17.410 align:middle line:84%
the cost of sellers was
assumed to be constant.

01:20:17.410 --> 01:20:22.240 align:middle line:84%
And the heterogeneity that you
observe in prices basically

01:20:22.240 --> 01:20:26.000 align:middle line:84%
maps to an unobserved
distribution of search costs.

01:20:26.000 --> 01:20:31.110 align:middle line:84%
So here you have to
disentangle the two.

01:20:31.110 --> 01:20:33.210 align:middle line:90%
So let me skip some of this.

01:20:33.210 --> 01:20:34.540 align:middle line:90%
I told you about this.

01:20:34.540 --> 01:20:37.890 align:middle line:84%
I just want to briefly show
you how the model works.

01:20:37.890 --> 01:20:45.000 align:middle line:84%
So we have buyers that have
heterogeneous search expenses,

01:20:45.000 --> 01:20:46.620 align:middle line:84%
and they can, like
in Hong and Shum,

01:20:46.620 --> 01:20:49.710 align:middle line:84%
decide how many draws to take
from the price distribution

01:20:49.710 --> 01:20:54.010 align:middle line:84%
or whether or not they're
using a broker instead.

01:20:54.010 --> 01:20:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And so because you know
the Hong and Shum model,

01:20:56.010 --> 01:20:57.570 align:middle line:90%
I can skip ahead a bit here.

01:20:57.570 --> 01:21:00.930 align:middle line:84%
So you get, again, a partition
of the search cost distribution,

01:21:00.930 --> 01:21:03.405 align:middle line:84%
but those with the very
highest cost, they're

01:21:03.405 --> 01:21:04.655 align:middle line:90%
going to use a broker instead.

01:21:04.655 --> 01:21:11.330 align:middle line:90%


01:21:11.330 --> 01:21:13.640 align:middle line:84%
So as before, we
want to keep track

01:21:13.640 --> 01:21:16.550 align:middle line:84%
of buyers that are
the mass of buyers

01:21:16.550 --> 01:21:17.820 align:middle line:90%
between these marginal types.

01:21:17.820 --> 01:21:19.620 align:middle line:84%
This gives rise to
these search weights.

01:21:19.620 --> 01:21:22.037 align:middle line:84%
So these are the buyers that
search m times from the price

01:21:22.037 --> 01:21:25.304 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

01:21:25.304 --> 01:21:30.020 align:middle line:84%
And so because the seller's
cost is also heterogeneous,

01:21:30.020 --> 01:21:32.930 align:middle line:84%
we don't get a mixed
strategy pricing equilibrium,

01:21:32.930 --> 01:21:35.180 align:middle line:84%
but we instead get
for any different cost

01:21:35.180 --> 01:21:38.570 align:middle line:84%
that a seller could
draw, we get a bidding

01:21:38.570 --> 01:21:43.795 align:middle line:84%
function that maps the seller's
unobserved cost to a price.

01:21:43.795 --> 01:21:47.840 align:middle line:90%


01:21:47.840 --> 01:21:50.540 align:middle line:84%
And so just to show
you this briefly,

01:21:50.540 --> 01:21:55.070 align:middle line:84%
I know I'm going fast here, but
we now get a profit maximization

01:21:55.070 --> 01:21:56.850 align:middle line:90%
problem that looks like this.

01:21:56.850 --> 01:21:59.720 align:middle line:84%
The sellers are maximizing
for any given consumer

01:21:59.720 --> 01:22:03.860 align:middle line:84%
that contacts them their margin
times the weighted average

01:22:03.860 --> 01:22:06.050 align:middle line:84%
across different consumers
that they could encounter

01:22:06.050 --> 01:22:07.425 align:middle line:84%
would take n draws
from the price

01:22:07.425 --> 01:22:10.460 align:middle line:84%
distribution times
the probability

01:22:10.460 --> 01:22:12.830 align:middle line:84%
that they're selling
to that consumer.

01:22:12.830 --> 01:22:15.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, what is different here is
that these marginal costs are

01:22:15.530 --> 01:22:18.710 align:middle line:84%
not uniform as before
as in Hong and Shum,

01:22:18.710 --> 01:22:21.140 align:middle line:84%
but they're drawn
from a distribution.

01:22:21.140 --> 01:22:24.650 align:middle line:84%
And so what's nice about
thinking about this problem

01:22:24.650 --> 01:22:30.770 align:middle line:84%
is that this actually
leads to a closed form

01:22:30.770 --> 01:22:33.830 align:middle line:84%
solution for the
bidding function that

01:22:33.830 --> 01:22:38.630 align:middle line:84%
is like in a first price
procurement auction.

01:22:38.630 --> 01:22:40.910 align:middle line:84%
I know we'll do auctions
later in this semester,

01:22:40.910 --> 01:22:44.780 align:middle line:84%
but this is a first
price procurement auction

01:22:44.780 --> 01:22:49.030 align:middle line:90%
solution to an IPV problem.

01:22:49.030 --> 01:22:51.480 align:middle line:84%
And this is essentially
just a weighted average

01:22:51.480 --> 01:22:56.100 align:middle line:84%
across what you would bid
if the number of competitors

01:22:56.100 --> 01:22:56.718 align:middle line:90%
were known.

01:22:56.718 --> 01:22:58.260 align:middle line:84%
And so here the
number of competitors

01:22:58.260 --> 01:23:00.230 align:middle line:84%
depends on the consumer
search strategy.

01:23:00.230 --> 01:23:03.300 align:middle line:84%
So just to wrap up and
put the pieces together,

01:23:03.300 --> 01:23:06.360 align:middle line:84%
so basically this kind of
takes the Hong and Shum model

01:23:06.360 --> 01:23:11.193 align:middle line:84%
and then introduces
heterogeneity on the supply side

01:23:11.193 --> 01:23:13.110 align:middle line:84%
and kind of maps this
essentially back to what

01:23:13.110 --> 01:23:14.940 align:middle line:90%
is an auction problem.

01:23:14.940 --> 01:23:17.140 align:middle line:90%
And I'll leave it here.

01:23:17.140 --> 01:23:21.380 align:middle line:84%
I'll probably tell those who
come to [14.]273 some more about

01:23:21.380 --> 01:23:21.880 align:middle line:90%
this.

01:23:21.880 --> 01:23:24.290 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, that's it for today.

01:23:24.290 --> 01:23:25.970 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:23:25.970 --> 01:23:38.000 align:middle line:90%